Spectacular "wingsuit jump" over Bogota
Sportsman Jhonathan Florez skipped from a helicopter on Thursday over Bogota, the Colombian capital.
Equipped with an wingsuit (a suit with wings), it rose to 160 km/h above the famous Monserrate sanctuary, located more than 3,000 meters, where many onlookers had rallied to observe its feat.
A black box in your car?
While U.S. road planners find it difficult to find the money needed to repair the decaying motorway infrastructure, there are many who see a solution in the form of a small black box that attaches itself above the dashboard of your car.
Devices, which record all bundles travelled by a motorist and pass on information to public officials, are at the center of a controversial attempt in Washington, D.C., and the government planning offices to reshape the outdated funding system for major United States routes.
The generally undifferenceless sector of major-the-shelf planning suddenly provoked a lively debate and turbulent alliances.
The libertarians joined environmental groups to press for the government to use the small boxes to keep track of the mileages you travel, and possibly the road on which you travel, and then use the information to draft a tax bill.
The Tea Party is appalled.
The American Civil Liberties Union is also very concerned and expresses its concern about the protection of privacy.
While members of congressionus cannot agree on whether to continue, several states have not waited.
They are looking at how, over the next decade, they will be able to move to a system that allows drivers to pay depending on how many mileages travelled.
Thousands of motorists have already boarded these black boxes, sometimes equipped with a GPS monitoring system, for an experimental ride.
This is really essential for our nation.
"It's not as if we had a choice," said Hasan Ikhrata, Managing Director of the Southern California Association of Governments, which predicts that the state will start recording the mile bundles travelled by every Californian driver by 2025.
There's going to be a change in the way we pay these taxes.
Technology is here to do it.
The pressure comes from the fact that the country's Highway Trust Fund, financed with the taxes that the Americans are pumping, is financially dry.
The United States no longer buy as much fuel as they did before.
Cars can ride more mile with a gallon.
The federal tax itself of 18.4 cents per gallon has not increased in the last 20 years.
Politicians are relucipated to raise the tax, even by 1 cent, while gasoline prices are high.
“The gasoline tax is simply unsustainable,” said Lee Munnich, a transportation policy specialist at the University of Minnesota.
Its state has recently installed trackers on 500 cars to test a mile payment system.
“This turns out to be the most logical alternative in the long term,” he said.
Wonks calls this mileage user fees.
It is not surprising that the idea seduces urban liberals, since taxes could be used to change driving habits so as to reduce congestion and gas emissions, for example.
California planners are interested in the system because they are developing strategies to achieve the goals set out in the state’s ambitious global-warming-modern laws.
But Representative Bill Shuster (R-Pa.), Chairman of the House Transport Committee, stated that he also regarded it as the most sustainable alternative in the long term.
Proponents of the Free Market of the Reason Foundation also appreciate the idea of making drivers pay per mile.
“This isn’t just a tax rushing into a big black hole,” Adrian Moore, Vice President of Politics at the Reason Foundation.
People pay more directly for the benefits available to them.
The movement is also supported by two former U.S. Transport secretaries who, in a 2011 report, urged congressionals to go on the path of payment mile.
The U.S.S. Senate has approved a pilot project of EUR 90 million last year which would have involved approximately 10 000 cars.
But the House presidency killed the proposal, addressing the concerns of rural gamblers representing electors, who had to travel many miles every day to go to work or in the city.
Nevertheless, several States and major cities are making progress on their own.
The most determined is Oregon, which mobilised 5,000 drivers to lead the most important experience in the country.
These motorists will soon pay kiss fees to the State instead of taxes on gasoline.
Nevada has already completed a pilot project.
The City of New York is ensuspecting one.
The Illinois attempted a test on a limited number of lorries.
And Coalition I-95, which includes 17 agencies involved in road transport in states along the American east coast (comprising Maryland, Pennsylvania, Virginia, and Florida) is investigating how it could implement the change.
The concept is not a universal success.
In Nevada, where about 50 volunteer motorists recently equipped their cars with an aircraft, drivers were worried that the government could keep track of every move.
“Worries about Big Brother and things are a big problem,” said Alauddin Khan, who heads strategic performance management at Nevada’s Department of Transport.
It's not something that people want.
From the beginning of the test, the Nevada ACLU warned on its site: “It would be relatively easy to turn these devices into full-blown location devices.”
There is no need to build an enormous and bulky technological infrastructure that will inevitably be expanded to keep the recordings of people's daily movements.
Nevada is one of a few states that are now struggling to find affordable technology that would allow the state to know how many miles a car, but without knowing the exact location or time.
If we can do that," added Khan, the citizens will be more comfortable.
Searching for this technology has led some state agencies to use a small California start-up called True Mileage.
Originally, the company was not intended to help motorists who pay taxes to the states.
It was trying to break into the emerging car insurance market, in which drivers would pay according to their kissing.
But the devices it is testing have plummeted for major-axis planners because they don't use GPS and provide a limited amount of information, downloaded periodically by modem.
“People will be more inclined to do that if you don’t record their speed or locate them,” said Ryan Morrison, Managing Director of True Mileage.
Major errors have been made in the pilot programmes of some States.
Much less costly means and much less intrusive methods exist.
In Oregon, the planners try the experiment by offering motorists different choices.
They can choose a device with or without GPS.
They may still choose not to have an appliance at all, preferring instead to pay a package based on the average number of mile mile rife travelled by all residents of the state.
Other states are hoping to sell the concept to distrusting citizens by allowing devices to do more, rather than not enough.
In the City of New York City, transport officials are seeking to develop a taxation system that would also be equipped to pay for parking fees, provide “driving payment” insurance, and create a real-time speed data set from other drivers that motorists could use to avoid junkitting.
“Mounters would be encouraged to participate because of the benefits that the system offers them,” said a document on urban planning.
However, some transport planners are wondering whether the whole mile payment debate is not just a huge distraction.
At the Metropolitan Transportation Commission of the San Francisco Bay Area, officials say that conundrum could very simply manage the bankruptcy of the Highway Trust Fund by increasing gasoline taxes.
An additional single or annual tax could be imposed on drivers of hybrid vehicles and others whose vehicles do not consume a great deal of gasoline, so that everyone pays its fair share.
“You don’t have to use radical surgery when you simply need aspirin,” Randy Rentschler, director of legislation and public affairs, told the committee.
If we do that, hundreds of millions of motorists will be worried about the protection of their privacy and a bunch of other things.
David Bowie: four unpublished tweeping
The English Musician doesn't stop surprising this year.
Starting with The Next Day, published in January, it concocted a luxury reissue scheduled for November 4, with several unpublished events.
Four have already esposhed on the Web.
Announced David Bowie's new album left everyone pungent.
