After ten years of quiet (the last album, Reality, dates from 2003) and too few appearances in public, the English musician proved that it could still be relied upon to scintill the pop scene.
A feast for its fans
Unsurprisingly, David Bowie had more than one ride in his bag with The Next Day.
The Thin White Duke plans to reissue the album on November 4.
For the occasion, he concocted a feast for his fans.
This re-edition, titled The Next Day Extra, will take the form of three album: the original album, unpublished studio sessions and remixes, as well as a DVD featuring the four clips already unveiled.
"The Next Day Extra" will contain a total of ten additional songs compared to the original album: the three songs of the Deluxe Edition, five songs specially unveiled for the occasion, as well as two remixes.
David Bowie also made the presentations of this beautiful box set in a video.
He introduces at the same time each of the discs, but also the accessories provided with: photos and exclusive covers, a notebook to share its own impressions, a libretto with lyrics...
And, above all, it gives a foretaste of its new title Atomica, typically in the vein of The Next Day, with very advanced guitars and cleverly mastered rock electricity.
Unpublished already Listening
But Atomica isn't the only title to unravel its charms.
The Informer, Like A Rocket Man, Born In A UFO are also available.
The Informer is double-facing: a worrisome intro before a luminous cavalcade that gradually slows down to make room for a pop ballad.
On "Like A Rocket Man", did Bowie want to make a small reference to Elton John's Rocket Man or even Gravity?
With this cheerleader, the singer at any rate seems when he no longer has his feet on the ground.
Space Oddity next door was much more serious.
On Born In A UFO, David Bowie refers once again to its strangeness: would it come from another planet?
Guitar riff, benevolent make you want to leave the Earth.
Bowie in any case amuses playing the chameleon with these songs: in turn an informant, man aboard a rocket, Martian potential...
It conceals and reveals at the same time plays to adopt different personalities, as he has done throughout his career, notably with his ziggy character Stardust and Aladdin Sane.
Not surprisingly he therefore had a mask in his hand in the Photography of The Invitation au Voyage campaign by the Louis Vuitton brand, whose new face he embodies.
It will appear in one of their advertisements, which will be broadcast from 10 November.
Defence Minister Rob Nicholson insisted that injured soldiers were not summarily released from the Canadian Armed Forces, and pointed out that a transition process was being followed by all soldiers before they returned to civilian life.
Attacked by Liberals and New Democrats in the House of Commons, Nicholson ensured that, prior to their release, the members of the army followed a transition plan in collaboration with their superiors.
All wounded soldiers receive proper care for their return to civilian life and none of them is released before being ready, he said.
Critics accuse the government of wanting to save money by not allowing injured soldiers - who do not comply with the "universal service" rule of the army, requiring that staff be able to perform a variety of tasks -- to reach the 10-year eligibility period required for retirement benefits.
In particular, they cited two cases reported by La Presse Canadienne, including that of a soldier released last Friday.
Corporal David Hawkins, a reservist from London, Ontario with post-Tiatraumatic Stress Disorder, was released from the military despite his request to stay one more year in order to receive a fully-indexed retreat.
His case came after Corporal Glen Kirkland, who told a parliamentary committee last month that he had been pushed out before he was ready because he did not meet the rule of universality of the service.
Mr. Hawkins pointed out that a soldier could be prepared to leave, with plans and consultation sessions, but that this was totally different from wanting to leave ranks.
"I told them I wasn't ready," he said in an interview with The Canadian Press on Wednesday.
I asked for months if there was a way for me to stay, and they didn't.
Since the onset of major fighting in Afghanistan, the army has struggled to determine what flexibility it can give to injured soldiers who want to remain within the ranks, but who are unsuitable for combat.
According to the current rules, severely injured soldiers have for up to three years to re-establish themselves.
If they do not meet the criteria for deployments abroad, they may be forced to leave the army.
Data presented last year to Parliament indicated that of the 1,218 soldiers released for medical reasons, 199 did not reach the length of service required to obtain pension benefits.
Liberal veteriled veteriled spokeswoman Jim Karygiannis on Wednesday for the reinstatement of Corporal Hawkins, while Neo-Democracy Jack Harris called for an immediate “shalegant practice.”
Disney betting on tablets to launch an animated serial
The U.S. Disney Media and Entertainment Group has decided to favor tablets on its own television channels for the upcoming release of a new children's series.
The first nine Episodes of Sheriff Callie's Wild West will be available as of 24 November on watchdisneyjunior.com or via its app for phones and tablets.
Its Global Disney Squancing launch was only scheduled for 2014, details the release of its Disney-June Division.
The cartoon, intended for children between the ages of 2 and 7, reministes the adventures of pussy Callie, the sheriff of a city in the American West where she reigns order with a magic lasso.
Each episode consists of two 11-minute stories.
"Interacting with Smart Smartphones and Tablets is second nature for children today," said Albert Cheng, Vice President for Digital Products at Disney/ABC Television Group.
This type of experience is part of Disney's efforts to "extend the lifespan of its series and build new relationships with its audience through increasingly important digital platforms," he added.
A poll published earlier this week by Common Sense Media showed an explosion in the use of mobile devices by young children in the United States: 38% of people under the age of 2 had already used a tablet or a telephone, and 72% of those under 8 years of age, compared to 10% and 38% respectively two years ago.
Snowden is ready to “cooperate” with Germany on US surveillance
Edward Snowden, the whistle-blower in the U.S. intelligence officer, said he was willing to go to Berlin to testify before the United States National Security Agency and its director Keith Alexander did not provide answers about their activities.
The German MEP Hans-Christian Strabele met with Mr Snowden in Russia on Thursday, where he has the right of asylum to discuss his testimonies in Germany.
In a letter that the honourable Member presented to the media in Berlin on Friday, Snowden said: “While the result of my efforts has clearly been positive, my Government continues to treat the difference of opinion as a leak and seeks to penalize political discourse with accusations against which it is impossible to defend itself.”
However, telling the truth is not a crime.
In his letter, Mr Snowden wrote that he believed that the support of the international community could persuad the United States Government to drop the criminal charges against it.
As part of the U.S. Department of Justice complaint, he is charged with espionage and theft of state property.
Hans-Peter Friedrich, the German Minister of the Interior, told Online: “While Mr Snowden is ready to talk to German officials, we will make that possible.”
Relations between the United States and Germany were strained following complaints that the NSA tweeted German Chancellor Angela Merkel’s mobile phone.
Thomas Oppermann, the Member of Parliament who is leading the parliamentary group overseeing foreign secret services, has indicated that if there is a chance to hear Mr. Snowden as a witness “without jeopardising him and completely ruining relations with the United States”, one should take advantage of it.
The German Green Member of the European People's Republic of Germany published a photo of him with Mr Snowden on his twitter account.
During his visit to Russia, he was accompanied by two German journalists.
He stated that, according to the lawyer of the former NSA consultant, Mr Snowden could not go back to Russia if he left the country.
If Mr Snowden testified in Germany, he would have to be assured that he would be “secure” there, the honourable Member said.
Mr. Snowden wrote in his letter that he had been subjected to a “serious and sustained” persecuting campaign that had forced him to leave his country.
However, he said he had been encouraged by the global response to “My act of political expression”.
Citizens from around the world as well as senior officials – including in the United States – have judged the revelation of an inexplicable system of permanent surveillance to be of public benefit.
The letter puts forward an offer of cooperation with the German authorities “when the difficulties of this humanitarian situation have been resolved”.
Cogeco Cable could soon offer interactive TV
Subscribers of Cogeco Câble may have access to apps such as Facebook, Twitter, and ultimately Netflix Video on Demand via their television in the not-short future.
Cogeco's subsidiary stated on Thursday that it was currently carrying out preliminary tests for the beta version of Cogeco with some of its users.
'This will allow us to highlight more user-friendly interfaces as well as more options,' Cogeco President and CEO Louis Audet explained in an interview.
Cogeco Cable thus followed suits its competitors, such as Bell, even if Cogeco's subsidiary does not yet have a precise date with regard to this new platform.
'We have to adapt and change or resist change and fail,' stressed Mr Audet.
The ultimate goal remains to offer tools to which our customers do not currently have access.
