Frontier Airlines plans to charge for hand luggage
Frontier Airlines is planning to charge up to EUR 100 for passengers carrying hand luggage on its flights.
Frontier Airlines is contemplating starting to charge up to EUR 100 for hand luggage and EUR 2 for a coffee or soda, although in its announcement on Wednesday it indicated that passengers could carry the unopened can with them when the plane is off-board.
The new cost of hand luggage refers to stowed sacks in the upper compartment, i.e. small sacks installed under the seat will always be free of charge.
Frontier has stated that it will charge EUR 25 if the charges are paid in advance and EUR 100 if the travellers only pay once at the boarding gate.
Frontier's spokeswoman, Kate O'Malley, stated that the EUR 100 was intended to encourage travelers to pay the fees in advance.
"We don't want to charge this price," she explained.
The airlines began charging for the first and second checked-confirmed luggage in 2008.
Passengers who were trying to avoid these charges put as many things as they could into hand luggage stored in the upper compartments, so there was often no room in these compartments.
These charges are a way of ensuring that passengers carry less things on board.
O'Malley has stated that these new expenses will not really be used to raise money.
The aim is to implicate the most loyal customers of Frontier that it is becoming more and more difficult to find a place in the upper compartments.
Passengers who buy their ticket on the airline's website will not have to pay.
For example, a passenger queuing at a Frontier gate could carry a sack free of charge, while the next person in the tail might have to pay 100 dollars for a similar sack.
O'Malley explained that Frontier's site and registration procedures are in the process of changing to ensure that passengers are aware of these charges before arriving at the gate.
Frontier Hand luggage will not be applied until the summer, although no date has been set.
Passengers often shudder about luggage and other charges, but airlines love them.
They believe that luggage-handling costs are high and that passengers who want luggage should pay for it.
Many people on Wall Street consider the addition of luggage charges as a sign that airlines charge enough to cover the cost of air travel after years of losses.
However, a large number did not receive luggage charges.
Spirit Airlines Inc. had spent the first hand luggage charges three years ago, and the low-cost airline Allegiant followed a little later.
The only other airline imposing such charges is the Hungarian company Wizz Air, airline consultant Jay Sorensen said, which closely tracks the costs at a surcharge.
In a December 2011 report, it estimated that Spirit's hand luggage charges amounted to EUR 50 million per year.
Sorensen, a former Midwest Airlines executive, recently traveled on Spirit and wondered what it was going to find at the boarding golf breeze when passengers would discover the unusuable personal baggage charges imposed by the airline.
"I've never seen such a fluid boarding procedure throughout my professional career," he said.
I was expecting to squeath teeth and a brawl sprinkle at the burgeon.
“The airplane was full,” he added, “passengers got on board at lightning speed.”
Frontier is also like Spirit, and sells for coffee, tea, soda or fruit juice.
Frontier has indicated that passengers who take so-downs or fruit juice may carry the unopened can with them, and those who take coffee can be squeezed free of charge.
It will always distribute water free of charge.
US Airways briefly attempted to charge for beverages in 2008 but returned back seven months later after the passengers complained and no other major airline had followed.
Frontier’s decision to charge a hand luggage fee when passengers do not buy their ticket directly from the airline is its latest effort to encourage customers to go to its site.
The airlines pay to online travel vendors such as Orbitz EUR 10 to EUR 25 for each ticket sold.
This prompted all airlines to encourage passengers to buy directly from them instead of contacting an online travel agency.
However, Frontier went a step further in this area.
In September, it began to don't offer every half of hundreds of loyalty to customers who were buying their tickets through an online travel agency.
On the other day, it reduced the mileage bounty by 25 per cent on a journey.
For example, a 1,000-mile Frontier trip purchased from an online travel agency would save 250 miles.
It also allows passengers to choose their seat in advance only if they purchase their ticket directly from the Frontier site.
Frontier has a loyal clientele in his hometown, Denver, but its business is shrinking and it loses money.
Revenues fell by 9% and its flight capacity fell by almost 13% in the first quarter, according to financial results released on Wednesday by its parent company, Republic Airways Holdings Inc.
Republic attempted to reshuffle the finances of Frontier by selling the airline.
N.A. implicates “internal error” not hackers for site failure
The Shadow National Security Agency stated on Friday that it was a bug that spawned its public website for a few hours, and not hackers as some had claimed online.
"NSA.gov remained inaccessible for a few hours this evening due to an internal error that took place during a planned update," the spy agency said in an email statement.
The problem will be solved tonight.
Allegations that the blackout was due to a distributed denial-of-duty (DSD) attack are falsifying.
Earlier this tonight, online server trackers noted that the NSA site had been broken down for at least 6 a.0 hours, and some users still cannot access the site.
Earlier, an NSA spokeswoman told ABC News that the agency's sensitive internal network was “not at all” compromised.
No classified information is in jeopardy, the Spokesperson explained.
At least one group of online hacktivists claimed to be responsible for the NSA shutdown by a DSD attack.
DSD Attacks aim to inundate a targeted site until the servers are overloaded and the site collapsates.
Cyber-tactical is not very sophisticated and attacks are not intended to penetrate the internal network of the targeted system.
The once ultra-secret NSA, once dubbed the “No Such Agency,” has found itself in the spotlight and strongly criticized in recent months following a wave of revelations about its vast communications surveillance programs at the national and overseas levels – which are part of the NSA secret files stolen from the agency and leaked by former NSA dismounted consultant, Edward S.
This growing controversy around the agency caused much speculation that tonight's incident was the result of targeted cyber-execution.
Downtown Delta now closed
Hôtel Delta Centre-Ville closed its doors on Thursday in Montreal, after 36 years of existence.
The edificing investment fund sold it to developers who would transform it into student residences.
Three hundred and a hundred and fifty people worked at the hotel.
Of these, there are still no new jobs.
Delta promises not to drop his employees.
Employers came to meet with employees directly on-the-spot, with individual employees meeting to assess their needs.
'This support will continue for the next six months,' explains Delta Regional Labour Relations Director Félix Bisson.
The closure of the Delta takes place in a very competitive hotel market.
The investment fund that was the owner of that building had choices to make.
Either it reinvested in the building to continue operating it, so there were investments of tens of millions while the competition is very fierce, there are a lot of new hotels that have appeared in Montreal.
