If Mr Montebourg succeeds the CGT and Goodyear to agree and Titan to buy the factory, we intend to stay in Amiens-Nord for more than four years.
Bombardier profit is declining as airplane deliveries and orders reced
Bombardier Inc, a Canadian aeronautical and rail manufacturer, reported a 15 per cent drop in its net profit on Thursday, under the pressure of lower orders and aircraft deliveries during the third trimester and contractual problems in its Train Division.
Bombardier, based in Montreal, did not provide in-flight test data for its entire new CSeries aircraft or information to determine whether the aircraft will adhere to its ambitious schedule of commercial entry into service before next September.
After the maiming flight of the test aircraft about a month and a half ago, the latter flew only three times, and the question then arose as to whether the test phase was well under way.
Results did not match expectations, and equities fell by 8 per cent on the Toronto Stock Exchange.
Cameron Doerksen, an an analyst at the National Bank Financial, lowered his rating on Thursday from “outperformance” to “sector performance” due to the low stock-execution growth potential over the next quarter or the next two quarters.
“Although the drop in airplane deliveries was largely anticipated, we are really disappointed by the profit margin in the transport division,” Cameron Doerksen said in a note to customers.
We believe that Bombardier will record new orders for its CSeries aircraft as the flight test program moves forward.
However, if no new orders are announced in the coming months, we expect the market to become more sceptical about the programme.
Bombardier hopes that the CSeries aircraft family will catapult it into the lower segment of a market now dominated by Boeing and Airbus.
The first test jet was unveiled in March and skyrocketed for the first time in September after months of delay.
But the number of firm orders for CSeries aircraft has so far been modest to the tune of 177. It seems that potential buyers are waiting for the results of in-flight tests to validate the company's claims regarding the energy efficiency and cost-saving potential of the new airliner.
At the present time, the company has registered a total of 403 purchase orders and commitments from 15 customers and operators.
Pierre Beaudoin, President and Chief Executive Officer, was convinced that Bombardier would reach its target of 300 firm orders before the commercial commissioning of the first aircraft.
Leaders also reassured analysts and the media on Thursday by telling them that the programme was moving forward according to the schedule.
“The test aircraft didn't stay on the ground longer than expected,” said Mr. Beaudoin at a conference call, adding that ground tests and software upgrades were planned during the aircraft shut-off time.
Each build-maker planned differently.
We had decided to carry out a first flight and to schedule an updating period, and that is what we did.
