It's already been five years since my dad erased from the world of the living.
At the very beginning I denied his dail, I was talking about him in the present.
I wasn't so scared of forgetting it, or rather I didn't know how I was going to keep 'frequetting'.
There are no recipes, no instructions for passing the wall of the invisible and retrieving one's own.
Then signs appeared.
For the first time it was the very strong feeling of its presence in the passenger seat as I was driving.
On another occasion, gentle waking up in the middle of the night to look at his watch which no longer leaves me, placed on the night table.
It is the image of my smiling father that accompanies me in my everyday actions.
Our mother left us after her assailing fight against cancer.
At the same time, this is what I was thinking about the day when I found his body emptied, shuddered on this hospital bed with wrinkled sheets.
Similarly, his funeral was an glacial farewell centered around the meagre gleam of candles surrounding its coffin.
I thought she was gone.
Gradually, in small diurnal or nocturnal appiness apparitions, a little timid, a little obliterated, she quickly came back into my mind, evolving as she regained its place in the landscape of my thought which seemed to mourled.
And then it turned out while unvelocating my own facets that I hadn't yet perceived, magnified by my relationship with her.
So I learned and understood that I didn't mislead my mother in any way but just a woman that I didn't really really know, a woman who embodied that person, during her time in life.
By dying, this woman had perfected her life and liberated the person I loved, and behold, I found her, full and complete.
This detour from the path of my life remains the most unforgivable and the most beautiful.
It's a privilege to know that beloved people never leave us.
I'm resembling my dad, "indoors and outdoors," it sounds.
I've always been told.
I didn't believe it at the time.
I had a difficult relationship with him until he became old, sick.
There, I wasn't afraid of him anymore and I could love him.
One day, he died.
For a long time, he accompanied me: when I stopped smoking, when I was scared, when I was in pain...
He was talking to me, constantly encouraging me, he indulging in my body.
I could see his hands as I looked at mine, lent my body to him.
But it was never indiscreet.
I could have had a personal life.
He gave me my intimacy.
It lasted for a long time and then one day he left.
Eventually, it was comfortable and reassuring to be understood, encouraged, advised.
I don't yet know who said that the deceased are not forgotten but invisible.
My parents no longer are, but I constantly feel them close to me.
Every event, every moment of my life makes me feel their presence because I always refer to it: what would they have said? What would they have thought? What would they have done?
I'm constantly dreaming of them, maybe not every night but several times a week it's for sure.
I often dream of the last moments that I have to share with them before it's too late, only, there is always one thing that prevents me from accomplishing this moment.
I waist often anxious when this happens to me, and I deeply feel their absence.
On the other hand, certain dreams come to me and fit into my story, and actually, they continue to live and share my life.
On the eve of my ultrasportical where I had to know the sex of my baby, I dreamed that I woke up, got out of bed, my father was waking me upstairs and smiling at me and rejoicing that I was waiting for a little boy.
The next day, I had the feeling that he had visited me in my sorrow to tell me that he happily shared what was happening to me.
And, of course, it was a little boy in my belly.
I loved sharing this moment with him and I take pleasure in revoking this common resemblance experienced after his death.
Just five years ago, my grandmother was dying of cancer.
A year earlier, she was traveling with my family in Cuba.
This is to mention the craction that his disappearance provoked among the young baccalaureate that I was at the time.
Nevertheless, I garnished my mourning quite quickly.
But it still influences my life, especially in the times when I find myself in trouble, or when I have to make important decisions.
Learning Arabic and interested in the Middle East, to the point of turning it into a master's degree in a high-school school, have not, hiddsued, been trivial choices.
In fact, I would often hear him speak Arabic in my childhood, and talk about Morocco, where it had lived for decades - before returning to France at the time of independence.
The values she inculcated to me are always present, so I sometimes think about how she wants me to act at some point.
The same is true of my attitudes: I try to live up to the generosity and righteousness of one who was a devivable believer.
From now on, I feel like a daily presence, a benevolent soul, a salvaging spirit.
I see his eyes resting on me.
My mother died nineteen years ago now.
Dead after talking to me on the phone.
I went through all phases: thrill, anger, pain, crying alone, anywhere, at any time, into unusual places, incongruous moments.
Then, the weather calmed down the effort.
There remained nothing but the lack, this unanswered emptiness, this need to confide me, to reassuring to the hollow of its gentleness.
Yet it's there, silent presence, it's there and it looks at me.
Every morning, I see his wrinkled eyes lurking upon me, I see the dark circles heavier his gaze heavier, the wrinkles around the lips dig by the cigarettes, the folds that mark the fore foreforest on days of concern.
My mother took possession of my face and she looks at me every morning in my mirror.
And every in the morning, I look away.
My wife and the mother of my three children died of cancer at the age of 43.
We constantly feel its protection, nothing serious can happen to us.
It was his promise on his bed of sorrow, so we gradually relearned to smile, and thrifting its name is no longer taboo but a solace.
Of course, I'm talking to her in the hollow of the night when the absence hurts too much, she invites herself into my dreams when cheerfulness is a little achieved.
We feel worn and protected in difficult times, the receding of time makes us realize how conductive she was, and we are trying to stay in the path that it had charted for us.
If a being has loved you very loudly, the absence cannot pull it out of your hearts and memories.
In a way, the beloved being becomes your inner energy.
I lost my dad on August 22, 2008 as a result of Asbestos cancer.
I was very close to him, I've always acted according to what he thought or what he liked.
I couldn't attend his burial, and three weeks after his death, gave birth to a little boy.
If I have my beliefs sometimes different from hers, I always wonder whether my actions are in line with his vision.
Indeed, I had problems in my work with these convictions.
For me it is impossible to go in the opposite direction, I do not blemish and I always wonder what he will think.
I don't know if I've embraced its way of being or whether I'm just like him: is it genetic?
In any case, it will always be my point of reference.
It wasn't a bit like an alter-ego, there wasn't a need to talk between us.
In short, it's here on a daily basis.
I feel its presence and it makes me happy.
It was almost thirty years since my husband died at the age of 33.
I was 28 years old and our six-year-would son.
The immense sorrow that then invaded me was of course ablated, but it is always close to me.
Quite very often "disgushell" in my dreams in such an accurate way, so vibrating than wawaking I'm even sadder to see that it was only a dream.
The other night, he asked me if I was all right, I told him no, he said, "I'm coming down," but in such a real voice that I woke up junkitting, worried and I lit the bedside lamp looking everywhere, convinced that it was going to happen.
I always live in the same house, and often, I feel observed and I turn around to see if he's not here, I know it's him and I'm talking to him.
In all the pieces I feel its presence and it makes me happy.
For nothing in the world I wouldn't leave this house where we were happily and where its spirit lives with me.
I'm 58 years old, I've always lived alone since his departure, it is and will remain the love of my life.
I want to make it clear that I'm sociable with a job of responsibility, I'm laughing and singing, I'm a grandmother, I have a lot of friends, but my heart and my soul belong to him and I never talk about him except with my son, and I never go to the cemetery.
