Cocanomane lawyer who informed a drug clerk from a police investigation was jailed
Basharat Ditta, aged 42, told crime lorr Neil Scarbrough
Lawyer feared that his secret drug addict will be brought to light
He was sentenced to three years'imprisonment by the Liverpool Assize Court
A major defence lawyer, who had informed an eldest drug sponsor of a major police investigation because he feared that his secret drug addiction would be revealed, was sentenced to three years'imprisonment.
Basharat Ditta, a 42-year-wouldriver, gave sensitive information to crime lord Neil Scarbrough concerning investigations of his drug-related activities after compromising himself as a result of his habit of consuming cocaine.
The lawyer, who was nicknamed “Bash” and regarded by the criminals as an “precipitable case”, was arrested at his home in 2011 as a result of police wiretapping to Scarborough, which he represented in a previous trial in connection with drug trafficking.
Police officers spotted Sarborough, aged 32, depositing three cocaine sacks at the lawyer's home in Blackburn, Lancashire, while he was attending an Bar Association dinner with colleagues.
The investigations revealed that Ditta was a “regular consumer” of this class A drug after tests showed traces of cocaine in her hair on his wallet and credit cards.
Over an eight-month period between January and August 2011, it sought illegal information about the arrest of two men on behalf of Scarborough as well as one of its partners.
The four suspects were monitored at the time by the police as part of a major investigation into heroin and cocaine trafficking taking place in Lancashire, Cumbria, Merseyside, Berkshire and West Yorkshire counties.
Together with 32 other men, they were subsequently incarcerated after the police seized heroin and cocaine for an amount of 1.5 million dollars, as well as more than 200,000 dollars in cash during a series of raids.
Ditta, aged 42, provided information to the criminals because he feared that his drug addiction would come to light.
Today, at Liverpool Assize Court, Ditta, who works at Forbes Solicitors in Blackburn Lawyer, has fallen into disgruntitude after being convicted on both counts of obstructing justice after a three-week trial.
He had admitted the possession of cocaine during a previous hearing.
The fall of the lawyer came after the police, practicing on Scarborough, discovered that he had been in regular telephone contact with Ditta in February 2011.
Two Investigators followed the suspect and spotted him arriving at Ditta's home and placing 60 per cent pure drugs under the garbage garb of the avocado hollow glove.
Shortly after the depot, Scarborough was in regular telephone contact with Ditta who was at a dining at Ewood Park Soccuping stadium, the Blackburn Rovers Precinctuated.
The lawmaker went back to his house, discovered the drugs and they telephoned nine times.
The court heard Ditta was a “regular consumer” of cocaine after tests showed traces of this class A drug in her hair, on her wallet and credit cards.
Ditta was arrested at a later time but denied consuming cocaine; he stated that he had spoken to the alleged drug dealer because it was his client and argued that their discussions were subject to a “legal privilege”.
In the course of his arrest, Ditta picked up his wallet and attempted to remove several credit cards, but they were all seized and a hair sample was taken.
During an interrogation, he stated that he owned an office at his home, as well as in the workplace, and that it would happen that clients would call him at home for legal questions.
However, the court heard testimonies that it was appealing to major players in the drug trade, some of whom it had depicted, after key arrests to tell them which investigators were aware.
Public Prosecutor Anne Whyte said, “If anyone needs to know not to violate the law, it is indeed a criminal lawyer.”
Mr. Ditta is accused of abusing his position as a criminal lawyer, and of having become too involved with particular clients.
The relationship that we are talking about is not just a drug dealer, but a drug trafficker supplying drugs to his own lawyer.
Some of its communications will undoubtedly be legitimate communications because it was his lawyer.
But this went far beyond the ordinary limits of a relationship between a lawyer and his client.
It obstructed the investigation of the police as much as it could to enable them to continue its criminal activities.
Mr Ditta did not honour his profession, but dishonoured him.
It came too close to certain customers, in particular Scarborough, and allowed its independence to be compromised.
Ditta denied any illegal action and said, “If I were a corrupt lawyer, what I am not, and if I had wanted to provide Mr. Scarborough with information, I wouldn't have waited until 15.0 p.m., I would have done it immediately.”
But after the hearing, Superintendent Lee Halstead of the Lancashire Police said: “M. Ditta has gone from a criminal lawyer to a criminal when he started buying drugs from criminal organizations.”
Its addiction to Cocaine had compromised it forever and made it vulnerable to ulterior the ulterior members of the organized criminal groups, which commissioned it to obtain valuable information about police investigations.
Lawyers should observe the highest standards of integrity and build public confidence.
Mr. Ditta betrayed this confidence and tried to hide behind the varnish of his profession.
The Lancashire Miscellaneous Organized Crime Unit conducted an Investigating Mr. Ditta, who had been found guilty on all three counts of possession of cocaine and now obstruction of justice, demonstrating its commitment to sue criminals.
Let this case serve as a warning to criminals so that they know that no one is above the law.
We'll find you and guide you to the courts.
Scarborough had himself been incarceration for 14 years after pleading guilty to aiding abeyance of trafficking in heroin, cocaine and cannabis.
Thirty-Fifther persons involved in trafficking were sentenced to a total of 153 years'imprisonment for drug trafficking.
