New application for collective action against the Sainte-Croix brethren
A new application for a collective action has been filed against the Saint-Croix Brother Brothers in connection with sexual assault allegedly taking place in St. Joseph's Oratory, as well as in several orphanages, colleges and schools.
The application is based on the complainant's testimony, identified as “J.J.”, which was allegedly masturbated in the 1950s, first at Notre-Dame-des-Neiges by his teacher, then by his confessor, Father Bernard, at the oratory of Saint-Joseph, where he served as a mass and where his father worked as a painter.
The first remedy concerned only three establishments, and when we announced the regulation, a lot of people have told us: 'I was in such a place, can I register?' says lawyer Alain Arsenault, who defends the victims in both appeals.
It is the particularity of the collective action that has just been filed to permit the addition of complainants from any institution where ill-treatment may have been perpetrated by members of the Congregation of Saint-Croix.
At the present time, the appeal contains the complaints of 25 persons who claim to have been assaulted by the Sainte-Croix brothers.
The alleged facts are generally older than those mentioned in the first appeal, since several of the establishments targeted closed in the 1960s.
The remedy also points out that the Congregation of St. Croix and the oratory of Saint-Joseph, which is a separate entity, “allowed sexual abuse against children”, that they “exercised moral, religious and psychological coercion against the victims”, that they were “knapped with the sexual abuse and nevertheless suffocated them”, and that they “consciously and consciously failed”.
These latest accusations are based, inter alia, on letters written by St. Croix brother's lawyer, Mr. Émile Perrin in the 1990s, as well as by the research carried out in the archives on this subject by Brother Wilson Kennedy, a former Brother of Sainte-Croix who publicly denounced the ill-treatment.
The class action must first pass the stage of admissibility in the Superior Court.
Once the admissibility has been accepted by the Court, a second step, i.e. hearings on the merits.
In the case of the first appeal, the Congregation of Sainte-Croix had agreed to settle amicably before hearings were conducted on the merits.
