“We want peace back; we want stability,” said Vanessa de Sousa, Managing Director of an investment company.
Worried about the future of her country, she traded her garments for a T-shirt marked “we want safety” in Portuguese before rejoining the crowd gathered in Independence Square of the capital on Thursday.
For two weeks, almost every day there were reports of conflicts between government forces and Renamo, the worst skirmishes since the peace agreement signed more than 20 years ago.
The Renamo was formerly a well-famous rebel movement, initially supported by Rhodesia governed by whites and the apartheid government of South Africa as part of efforts to destabilize the self-contained government.
After a peace agreement signed in 1992, it became an opposition party.
Analymists believe that the country should not fall back into an open conflict, but recent events have shaken foreign investors and the local population.
The challenge is considerable for the burgeoning economy, as the discovery of huge reserves of offshore gases and coal deposits in the north-western region could attract more than EUR 50 billion in investment over the next few years from companies such as Rio Tinto, Vale of Brazil, Eni of Italy and Anadarko of the United States.
Frelimo, the party in power and the main political force since 1975, and the Renamo blame each other for tensions.
The Renamo stated that the government was responsible for the recent clashes and had launched an attack on its members in the province of Sofala, a traditional bastion of Renamo on 17 October.
The assaults on the former rebels degenerated when government forces attacked Renamo bases and attempted to kill Afonso Dhlakama, the leader of the group, and Fernando Mazanga, Renamo spokeswoman, according to the Financial Times.
The government made Renamo responsible for unleashing the conflicts, accustomed it to attack soldiers.
President Armando Guebuza sought to minimize concerns about instability.
Guebuza told AFP, the French news agency on Wednesday, that Dhlakama regarded himself as a “loser” who wanted to use “all the forces he had left in an attempt to prove that it could impose its decisions on the government”.
Frelimo and Renamo insisted that they both wanted to avoid the war.
But concern grew after Mr. Mazanga stated that Renamo was abandoning the 1992 peace agreement.
He told the FT that he meant that the deal was no longer respected by Frelimo.
“We want to resume the negotiations, but with the utmost seriousness,” said Mazanga.
Previous discussions between the parties have not succeeded in appeasing tensions fuelled by a series of conflicts this year.
“It is two tall men (Guebuza and Dhlakama) who keep arguing,” explained Joseph Hanlon, an open-university lecturer who specializes in Mozambican.
None of them knew how to negotiate well, and none of them was prepared to make the necessary concessions.
Renamo, which is in favour of electoral reforms, had already stated that it would boycott the municipal elections to be held in November.
Presidential and legislative elections are scheduled for next year.
Some commentators have interpreted its use of force as an attempt by a sick movement to obtain concessions and a treasury of war from the government.
Renamo's share of the vote has been steadily declining since 1992, while a new party, the Democratic Movement of Mozambique (MDM), which was created by a former Renamo member, is expected to obtain a greater number of votes in the elections.
Mr Mazanga has said that Guebuza – who should step down at the end of its second term next year – wants to destroy democracy in the country.
“He doesn't want a multi-party democracy, he doesn't want transparent elections, he doesn't want peace because he doesn't want to leave the presidency," said Mazanga.
We don't know exactly what the Renamo's capacity is, but it has carried out disruptive light attacks on police stations and vehicles on a major north-south highway.
Most skirmishes took place in Sofala province, which is several hundred kilometers north of Maputo, but where Beira is, the port from which mining companies, including Rio Tinto and Vale, export the charcoal.
In June, Rio was no longer able to use the railway for about a week after Renamo threatened to attack the railway line.
Mr. Mazanga was mischievous when he had been asked whether Renamo would repeat that threat.
Renamo wanted to “know the international community that things are not going well in Mozambique,” Mazanga said.
Unstability has deepened government frustration, says Fernando Lima, the director of Mediacoop, an independent media company, with many also being concerned about corruption, the slow pace of development and the recent wave of abductions.
