Ceremonies in Memory of Creatized Deceased
At 11 a.m. The crematorium du Père Lachaise organized a secular ceremonies at 11 a.m. in the memory of all the deceased cremated in its establishment during the year.
Anne Hidalgo, a socialist nominee for mayor of Paris, is to attend, and will then explain, at a press conference, his proposals on funeral.
To follow the ceremony, click above.
For their own funeral, more and more French people are choosing cremation, rather than burial: 53%, according to an Ipsos study carried out on 6 and 7 September among 1009 people, instead of 47%.
For the funerals of a relative, this is the opposite: the French prefer burial (53% versus 47%).
If they have the misfortune of losing a child, only 15 per cent chose cremation.
In his book Dead in ashes, Damien Le Guay, philosopher and Vice-Chairman of the National Funeriary Ethical Committee, emphasizes the "Violence" of cremation for survivors.
There is cremation, with cremation, 'violation done to the beloved body', which will be 'reduced to a heap of ash' in a very short period of time, and not after a decomposition process, which "wouln't accompany the garment phases".
There is also "symbocilic violence", due to 'extraction of singularity and distinctive tales' reduced to "anonymity" of the ashes.
Why is what is accepted without difficulty in certain Nordic and Protestant countries is still poorly experienced in France?
"Because cremation is a new fact," replied Marie-Frédérique Bacqué, President of the Society of Tomatology and Author of the book Apprivoizing Death.
It has only been tolerated since 1963 by the Catholic Church, which has limited attempts to tame it.
Appriving cremation is for François Michaud-Nérard, director-general of the Funeral Services of the City of Paris, planning for the defunct a ceremony as dignified as the one to which he would be entitled with burial.
Ipsos' annual survey shows a very strong commitment to the organization of the ceremony.
7 7% of the French want one, for their loved ones, whether religious (53 per cent) or civil (24 per cent).
From now on, 73% of people who have chosen cremation want to organise a ceremonial.
6 per six per cent of atheists or non-believers also want it.
Crematoriums have been adapted to this evolution of society.
'For about ten years, they have been trying to prevent the families from abusing an expectation, without doing anything for one and a half o'clock, immediately followed by the ash,' observes Michaud-Nérard.
Seventy-seven per cent of the institutions now offer masters of ceremonies, in the presence of the body, performing the following racing: receiving the assembly, naming the deceased, linking it to others, evoke it, giving meaning to his death, organizing the farewell.
This has been the case at Père Lachaise crematorium since 1998.
Ceremonial teachers are often people who have reconfigured.
There are also former Catholic priests.
It is in this context that Père Lachaise crematorium, since 2010, on the day of La Toussaint, has been organizing several ceremonies of remembrance, secular and non-religious, to which it invites the families of people who have been cremated throughout the year.
