Ben Greenman: Tenth Anniversary of the New York Comedy Festival: The New Yorker
It could be said that the city of New York is the place where Stand-up was born in America: almost 100 years ago vaudeville actor Frank Fay, who acted as master of ceremonies at the Palace Theatre on Broadway, started telling jokes directly in front of the audience on the tone of the conversation.
Fay’s innovation has continued over the years, until more recently picked up by the New York Comedy Festival.
Created and supervised by Caroline Hirsch, the founder of Carolines stand-up club, the festival celebrates its tenth anniversary this year, with more than 60 shows presented in small clubs and large theatres.
“Most of the headliners emerged in Carolina, and were very successful, until they became too famous to play in a club,” said Hirsch.
We created this festival in order to be able to continue to work with them.
This year's event will include Wanda Sykes, Kathy Griffin and Bill Maher and "Stand Up for Heroes", an evening of comedy and music for veterans at Madison Square Garden, to include Bruce Springsteen, Jon Stewart, Roger Waters and Bill Cosby.
