Challenging the Right to Abortion in Abortion in Texas
A Federal Appeal Court re-enclined in Texas certain restrictions on the right to voluntary termination of pregnancy that had been blocked by a trial judge this week.
Such a judgement meant that a piece of legislation adopted in that State in July and largely calling into question the right to abortion could enter into force.
The judgement handed down by the Court of Appeal means that doctors who practice abortions will have an “admission privilege” to local hospitals.
Advertising privilege is the right of a medical practitioner, by virtue of his status as a caregiver of a hospital, to admit a patient into a hospital or a medical centre in order to provide a diagnosis or treatment.
The defenders of abortion argue that the content of the law may result in an immediate closure of one-by-third of state clinics, as these clinics have not succeeded in obtaining this "advertising privilege" for their practitioners.
Altogether nearly 22,000 women would be denied access to these establishments.
On Monday, a judge of first Instance stated on the eve of the entry into force of the law that the provisions dealing with admission privilege were unconstitutional.
But the Texas Attorney-General, Republican Greg Abbott, who runs the office of governor, asked the Court of Appeal to overscind the first instance judgement that blocked the application of the law.
A plenary hearing on this issue is scheduled for the month of January.
