Court blocks NYPD Control and Search Decision
A Federal Appeals Court on Thursday blocked a judge’s decision demanding changes to the New York Police Department’s control and search program and removed it from the record.
The United States Circuit (second Circumstance) stated that Judge Shira Scheindlin's decision was suspended until the completion of an appeal filed by the city.
The judge had ruled in August that the city had violated the Constitution following the implementation of its programme to monitor and interrogate citizens.
The city appealed against its findings and corrective orders, including a decision to assign an auditor to assist the police department in changing its policy and its associated training programme.
The Court of Appeal heard the arguments relating to the request for suspension on Tuesday.
The appellate court stated that the judge should be removed from the file because she breached the judges' code of conduct by jeopardizing the need for a judge to avoid the appearance of partiality, in part because of a series of media interviews and public statements in which she publicly reacted to court critics.
The judge had ruled that the police officers violated the civil rights of tens of thousands of people by mistakenly targeting black men of Hispanic origin as part of their control and search programmes.
It designated an outdoor controller to oversee the changes, including the reform of policies, training and supervision, and ordered the establishment of a pilot programme to test body-mounted cameras in neighbourhoods with the largest number of controls taking place.
In August, the City of New York had agreed to put an end to the practice of storing the names and addresses of persons whose cases were rejected after police screening.
The hearing of the appeal by the city is scheduled to take place after 14 March 2014.
The tactics of control and diaping have been criticized by many civil rights activists.
This system of control and diaps has existed for decades in some form, but the number of controls recorded increased dramatically under the administration of independent mayor Michael Bloomberg, reaching its highest historical level in 2011 with 684,330 controls that mainly concerned black and Hispanic men.
Judicial action was taken in 2004 by four men, all from minorities, and turned into collective action.
Proponents advocating changes in the NYPD’s monitoring and search program say that they will put an end to unfair practices, lead to more effective and trusted police forces, and will be able to change the way police departments use politics.
Opponents say these changes would undermine the morale of the police without decelerating criminality, would be a waste of money and would not solve the broader problem of police forces under pressure after the strength of police officers was drastically reduced over the last decade.
The judge pointed out that it did not put an end to the practices of monitoring and retrieval, which were constitutional, but was reforming the NYPD to implement its controls.
