Barack-Bagro receives Iraqi Prime Minister ambitantly blazing
The U.S. President was scheduled to receive Iraqi Prime Prime Minister Nuri Al Maliki on Friday, November 1, 2013, in search of U.S. assistance to fight the heaviest wave of violence in five years.
In October, Iraq experienced its deadliest month in five and a half years, according to figures published by the Iraqi authorities on Friday, 1 November.
9,964 people died in the violence in October, including 855 civilians, 65 police and 44 soldiers, and 1,600 were injured.
The United Nations has an even higher death toll with 1,979 deaths and 1,902 injuries.
This violence is increasingly lethal, despite strengthened security measures and large-scale military operations launched for months by the Shia-dominated Government of Nouri Al Maliki.
The total death toll in October is highest since April 2008, when 1,073 people were killed.
About two years after the withdrawal of US troops, the level of violence raises fears of further conflagration while neighbouring Syria is in the thrilled of a civil war.
Bombes hit markets, mosques, weddings and funeral.
Men are being shot in the streets or even at home, and security forces are also the target of frequent attacks.
The growing discontent of the Sunni minority in power under Saddam Hussein, complaining of being politically marginalised and of being the target of unjust arrests, has fostered this outbreak of violence.
On Friday, further violence lobbied four people in northern Iraq, in the wake of the deaths of at least 26 people in a series of attacks, including the explosion of five car bombs north of Baghdad.
Much of the violence was attributed to the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL), an al-Qaeda-affiliated (Sunni extremists), also involved in the civil war in Syria.
Nouri Al Maliki wants a "global war" against al-Qaeda.
The violence is at the center of talks in the United States of Prime Minister Nuri Al Maliki who will be received on Friday by President Barack Obama, two years after their previous meeting on 12 December 2011.
At the time, the US president, who was elected on the promise to end the military engagement in that country, gave an optimist picture of the situation.
Since arriving in D.C. on Wednesday, Nouri Al Maliki has pronudated meetings with the executive and elected representatives of Congress.
On Thursday, he pleaded for the international community to wage a "Three World War" against Al-Qaida.
The principle of increased security assistance to Iraq is supported by influential Republican and Democratic senators.
But the latter have also criticizing Nuri Al Maliki, a Shiite, attributing him partial responsibility for the resumption of violence through his “sectarian and authoritarian politics”.
They also required Barack Obama to convey to Nouri Al Maliki that "the pernicious influence of Iran within the Iraqi government constitutes a serious problem in our bilateral relationship".
