China’s head of internal security on Friday accused a group of Xinjiang Uighur separatists of being hudddled with the car bomb attack that killed five in Tiananmen Square in central Beijing on Monday.
The vehicle, an SUV, rushed into the mob in the notor-occupancy of the Chinese capital Square, a symbol of deadly repression in 1989, and caught fire killing its three passengers and two passers-by.
Meng Jianzhu, a member of the Political Bureau for Internal Security, accused the East Turkestan Islamic movement of being the instigator of this attack.
Many Uighurs, a Turkish-speaking minority settled in Xinjiang, call this Chinese province East Turkestan.
