Beijing challenges Turkmen Islamic movement
'Behind-scenes support for the attack came from East Turkestan Islamic Movement based in Central and Western Asia,' the head of the Chinese security organs told a Hong Kong television station, according to a video posted on Thursday evening on a website.
Meng Jianzhu, a member of the Political Bureau of the Chinese Communist Party (CPC), spoke in Tashkent during an official visit to Uzbekistan.
This is the first time that a Chinese official designates a particular organization after Monday's attack.
According to the Chinese police, three Uighurs from the same Muslim-dominated Xinjiang family, bordering several Central Asian countries, prepulsed their car loaded with gasoline dishes against the Banned City entrance to Beijing, in a suicide attack that resulted in two deaths and 40 injuries.
The driver of the car, his wives and his mother perished in the burning of the car.
The Etim, which claims to be fighting for the independence of East Turkestan, the former name of Chinese Xinjiang, was classified by the United Nations in 2002 as affiliated with al-Qaeda.
This movement is often identified by the Chinese authorities as being responsible for sporadic disturbances in Xinjiang, but its real influence is questioned by several experts.
George Kerevan: Disruption of Europe Gives Scots Choices
On another day, another worrisome history of independence.
This time, we are being warned that an independent Scotland would be obliged to join the European area of free movement in order to be able to join the EU.
And lobbies we're hearing passport control stories in Berwick and a barbed-wire border along Hadrian's wall.
While Strathclyde’s diary indicated the possible economic impact of greater freedom of freedom of movement with the rest of Europe, it did not appear in the headlines (and this was expected).
Nor has anyone said that EU Member States spend a large proportion of their time circumventing their formal rules when it suits them.
Since Scotland is not part of the Schengen area for the time being, continued non-observance of the rules would be a sparse concession that Brussels could offer in return for everything it wants from the Scotians.
So it's a story that doesn't.
And a story so old that it's now bloomed: I first heard the bobbler that "independence means passport control" more than 40 years ago.
Yet we have forgot an interesting point in this interpretation of an old dusty tale.
Why should an independent Sceatic follow Europe's instructions, in fact?
Why exchange the yoke of London for that of Brussels, in particular now?
This is the real situation with regard to Europe: the great post-war plan to unify Europe is definitively stalled.
With the crisis of the euro, Project Europe is officially dead.
Across Europe, parties that are opposed to the EU or are campaigning for the abandonment of the euro as a common motto are gaining ground.
Even in Germany, the Euro-Sicpice Alternate for Germany, founded only this year and came from nowhere, garnished almost five million votes in the September federal election and pulled out the Liberal Democratic Party (equivalent of our Liberal Democrats) in the Bundes.
There has always been a national opposition to the proposed creation of a federal Europe.
However, the current economic crisis marked a turning point.
Authorization imposed by Berlin and the European Central Bank, coupled with the yoke on national economies through joining the single-currency, has led many people to believe that Project Europe has gone too far.
The euro crisis has little to do with national governments with excessive budget deficits – this was only true for Greece.
Instead, the European system has raised exchange rates favourable to Germany’s exporters – what German politicians want to retain.
Without the possibility of devaluation of the national currency, the countries of southern Europe end up with a problem of intrinsic productivity vis-à-vis Germany.
The only resort is to make drastic cuts in salaries and public spending – encouraged by Berlin.
In addition to the current budgetary and monetary problems, there is a deeper malaise linked to productivity in Europe.
As a result of “green” energy policies imposed by Brussels – which are a reason for subsidizing French and German companies in the energy sector at consumer costs – European industry pays twice as much for electricity and four times for gas than the United States.
This is a crippling disadvantage in terms of costs, as we have already seen in Grangemouth.
All wage freezes in the world will not prevent the European petrochemical industry from being penalised by an inexpensive American shale gas.
As a result, the revolt rumble, in particular in France, one of the great leaders of the EU.
After the war, the French political eldestfounded the EU as a means of controlling Germany and putting Paris on an equal footing with Washington.
But Berlin no longer needs Paris as a passport of its political legitimacy and has imposed its own economic policy in Europe, letting the French economy scramble.
As a result, Marine Le Pen’s far-right party, the Anti-European National National Front, has just won a crucial by-election, returning socialists to power in third place.
The National Front is now the most popular party in France with 24% of the vote – a timely warning for the British Labour Party, which reminds it that it cannot assume that a split by the right will automatically favor the left.
What is Marine Le Pen doing with its new popularity with the French white working class?
It wants to use next year's European elections to create a single anti-murvious and anti-European bloc in the European Parliament.
If the anti-European parties look good at these elections, which is very possible, such a bloc could control the European Parliament for the first time.
This is what I am going to think very soon, the unique anti-European and anti-currency sentiment in Europe will unite and eventually kill the euro.
The EU will not disappear, but it will come back to something that will look more like “Europe of Nations (sovereigners)” favoured by General de Gaulle.
Germany and some of its satellite economies could retain the euro, but France and southern Europe will return to their own currencies.
I expect the United Kingdom to distancing itself from this project, and I hope that it will make a gentle eye in the United States.
However, D.Whizad’s growing interest in the Pacific suggests that Great Britain will be left behind in the Atlantic.
What about Scotia in all of this?
We can decide to be an area of Little England (essentially).
Or we can defend our own economic interests, which includes sending a walk between Berlin and Brussels.
I believe that Scotland could deal with a freer European agreement provided that we retain our own currency.
Co-operation with other like-minded countries would be easier in a Non-Fundamental United Nations Europe.
Otherwise, we should emulate Norway and maintain our economic independence.
The SNP government of Scotland is – and this is worth noting – the most successful anti-austereity political movement in Europe, winning a spectacular majority in 2011 based on its opposition to the dark cuts proposed (and implemented) by Labour Chancellor Alistair Darling, and the subsequent conservative/liberial-democrat coalition.
It would now be lully for Scotland to vote in favour of independence in order to accept the Austerity imposed by Berlin and Brussels.
Early puberty: Ageing earlier
Studies show that African-American and Hispanic girls tend to reach puberty age earlier than white girls.
Physic changes don't mean that puberty is looming
There is no evidence that hormones or other chemicals are responsible
Experts believe that the obesity epidemic could be a trigger for early puberty
The trend towards early puberty isn't as pronounced among boys
Former CNN Correspondent Pat Etheridge is a journalist specializing in children's family and health issues.
Must a mother have to worry if her daughter's magnificent breasts and hair start growing at the age of seven or eight?
At this week's American Academy of Pediatrics Conference in Orlando, Florida, Dr. Paul Kaplowitz, a pedo-endocrinomologist, explained that these early physical changes are quite common among young Americans and represent a new norm.
“I spend a lot of time reassuring the parents – in general, this doesn’t indicate a rapid shift to full puberty,” said Kaplowitz.
Obvious signs of development, such as budding the buds of the bristles, under the arms and in the pubic area and body odours, appear earlier in girls.
But there has only been a slight step forward in the age of early rule over the past four decades.
In the United States, the average age is 12-and-a-half, whereas it was two-half six-quarters in 1970.
“Once the breast has started to develop, it takes at least two or three years before the first periods appear,” said Kaplowitz, also the author of “Early Puberty in Girls: The Essential Guide to Coping with This Common Problem.”
Weather is the most accurate test to know how puberty will evolve.
There is a debate about what constitutes the actual appearance of puberty, but it is considered "early" when the development of the breast is accompanied by sudden growth before the age of 8.
In most cases, development slows or stops – this is something a pediatrician can follow closely.
Furnigrest development may justify tests carried out by an endocrinologist to eliminate serious problems such as tumors or kystas.
There are treatments to delay the appearance of early menstruation and to prevent other consequences: premature aging of the bones that can slow growth and a small waist in adulthood.
Drug and hormonal treatment recommendations are based on child age, developmental rate, growth rate and emotional maturity.
Psychosocial aspects are also important.
Kaplowitz was careful with drugs, but acknowledged that "controlling puberty can alleviate certain behavioral problems and the sensation for young girls to be different from others."
The other big problem is understandable: Parents simply don't want their very young girls to have menstruation.
“They fear the risk of pregnancy or even the manner in which they will manage hygiene issues,” said Kaplowitz.
"It's been a clash," recalls a woman whose first menstrual period at the age of 10.
Even if there were signs and we talked about the rules, it wasn't prepared emotionally.
She came back from school frightened and upset to be the first among her friends.
There are many theories brought to the attention of the public regarding the causes of early puberty.
However, there is no consistent theory according to which hormones contained in milk or other foods, chemicals in the environment or sexual messages conveyed by the media are responsible.
Boys, such as girls, reach puberty sooner.
Kaplowitz claims that the most credible hypothesis is the obesity epidemic.
It participated in a study of girls aged between 6 and 9 which links the percentage of fat to the onset of puberty.
Other discoveries support this conclusion, but there are many other influential factors.
In that country, Afro-American and Hispanic girls tend to reach puberty age earlier than white girls.
There are a number of explanations.
On a global scale, early puberty mechanisms seem to be influenced by everything from economic to climate to genes.
Another problem: Although boys have hairs on their faces and the younger pubic area, the trend towards genuine early puberty is not as pronounced as among girls.
Other doctors participating in the AAP conference stressed the complexity of the topic.
The appearance of acne and pubic hair is common, even in infants and toddlers.
“We need to be careful about the way we identify the true onset of puberty,” said Dr. Lawrence Silverman, a pedo-endocrinomologist working at Goryeb Children's Hospital in Morristown, New Jersey.
Parents should not hesitate to seek advice from their pediatrician on how to talk to their child.
“That may mean having a conversation earlier than expected,” explained Kaplowitz.
If you stay quiet, your child will generally react well.
Girls whose puberty is early must be reassured of the fact that, even when it comes sooner than expected, the process is part of life.
