'I'm on a permanent basis (Afghan Afghanistan, Syria, ...) before I leave," added Gérard de Villiers.
On the spot, I meet journalists, including AFP reporters, diplomats, people of the services that I've known for some for twenty or 30 years.
As a result, many of its SAS are often prejuncious: for example, a month before the attack on a Syrian regime command centre having killed several senior officials, he told the story in "Damastorm's Way".
In "Les Fuss de Benghazi", it was the first to reveal the existence of a secret CIA command center in this city, cradle of the Libyan revolt.
In 1980, it staged the assassination of Egyptian President Anouar El-Sadate in "Cairo Conspiracy", a year before the attack.
In October 2012, in "Panique in Bamako", it recounted columns of 4x4 of jihadists who were based on the Malian capital.
Gérard de Villiers defended, I simply make hypotheses from countries that I know very well and, from time to time, some of my hypotheses come true.
After these trips to often troubled areas, he sat down for a month behind his 1976 daisy IBM typewriter typewriter "whose coins have been changed".
300 pages later, he wrote the word "FIN" and corrected each page to the pen.
At the walls of his desk are hung AK-47 assault rifles, erotic photographs and photos of the author with African war lords.
Regularly pinned for machismo by feminist leagues and for racism by human rights organizations, Gérard de Villiers dismissed these accusations in two sentences.
Some women are sex objects in my books, but others are beautiful, intelligent and courageous women.
I am always well received in Africa, where I have so many readers.
Gérard de Villiers had said it: 'Malko Linge, like all heroes, has no age.
He won't die or retire.
Nor do I.
Aircraft manufacturers quarrel about the width of the seats while large controls are involved
The quarrel raged between the major aircraft manufacturers over the width of tourist-class sieves on long-haul flights, paving the way for a bitter confrontation at the month-long Dubai Air Show.
The conflict concerns the breadth of seats on long-haundor flights to non-ly-class passengers – which are not always the most worn by the airlines, but to which the space allocated is essential to increase efficiency gains in the latest aircraft presented by Airbus SAS SAS and Boeing Co.
This week, Airbus called on the aeronautics industry to put in place a standard imposing a seat size of at least 18 inches (45.72 cm) in economic classes, but its great American rival Boeing states that it should be for the airlines to decide.
The dispute erupted when aircraft manufacturers were trying to sell even larger versions of their twin-enhanced long-haul aircraft, possibly hoping for a record order at the event from 17 to 21 November.
The manner in which seats will be arranged at the rear of the aircraft – in particular, there will be 9 or 10 front seats – is essential for the economic efficiency of the “mini-jumbo segment”.
Boeing stated that its "777X" re-arranged could accommodate 406 people in seats more than 17 in inches wide, and would be configured with 10 seating positions per row.
Airbus indicates that the competing version of its A350 will transport 350 people in 18-inch-wide seating positions configured in rows of 9.
Aeronautics giants often exchange bets on technical issues by advertising in the professional press.
Today, Airbus appeals directly to the public before the Dubai Aeronautical Exhibition, where 777X should take precedence over its competitors with more than 100 orders.
We have recently realized what could be the beginning of a new advertising war with the presentation to the financiers of a photo showing three people against each other at the restaurant, entitled "Wouln't you accept that?"
“Boeing offers long-haul flights in narrower seats than regional turbo-propelled planes,” said John Leahy, Airbus Chief Commercial Officer.
As eating habits are changing, people grow, but sieves in airplanes don't change dramatically.
Between the beginning of the 1970s, when the Boeing jumbo 747 defined the modern long-haul journey, and the turn of the century, the weight of the average American from 40 to 49 years increased by 10%, according to data from the U.S. Department of Health.
The waist circumference of the average American of the twenty-first-century is 39.7 inches, according to US health statistics.
Airbus stated that its rival clung to a siege concept dating from the 1950s, when the circumference of the device repulsively dubbed "jet set" was narrower.
Airbus added that it had ordered a study which indicated that an additional thumb in the width of the seats would improve sleep quality by 53 per cent.
Boeing contests Airbus figures as far as seating width and states that it is not for manufacturers to decide how the airlines balance ticket prices and equipment.
It also states that the study shows that cabin experiment does not depend solely on the width of the seats.
“It’s really about bringing flexibility to the airlines and enabling them to do what they think they need to do to carry out their business,” said Kent Craver, Boeing Passenger Satisfaction Manager.
They don't want to be told what will allow them to be cost-effective.
They know their business better than anyone else.
For passengers, it is more a question of having some freedom of movement, but for suppliers it is more and more an issue that could affect their results.
Behind the quarrel lobbies lurehled is an aircraft order race for an estimated amount of at least EUR 700 billion at the market price in the coming decades, enough to tweeting the balance of American and European exports.
As Reuters first pointed out in July, siege configuration is exactly what fueled the battle between the latest aircraft.
Airbus and Boeing both claim seat-by-seat efficiency in their latest twin-engined long-haul models 20 per cent higher than that of the market-leader in this segment, the 365-seat Boeing 777-300.
The performance announced by Boeing depends in part on the comparison between the 777X configured with 10 seats per row and an original 777 model conjugated in ranks of 9.
Gain in terms of unit costs is small compared to the configured device with 10 seats per row currently in operation.
"Boeing does this to be able to house more seats and make its airplanes more Compensate to our products," said Kevin Keniston, Director of Comfort Passenger at European Airbus.
On the other hand, annotasists say cabin configured with 10 seated seats in the existing 777s suggest that many passengers vote in favor of a denser configuration, which can go hand in hand with cheaper fares.
“An 18-inch sieve width would be perfect for passengers, but the reality is that, from a commercial point of point of view, Airbus proposal is motivated by the 777 threat,” said interior cabin expert Mary Kirby, Founder and Editor-in-Chief of Runway Girl Network.
Airbus and Boeing did not provide seats, but offered a catalogue of suppliers from which airlines had to choose.
Airplane sellers who travel around the world even take one meter with them to check competing configurations.
While provoking to offer comfortable devices, all manufacturers also offer high-density aircraft to low-cost airlines and for regional voyages.
Airbus offers an A350 configured with 10 seats per tidy but indicates that it has not yet sold it.
Until recently, Airbus stressed the need for greater cabin personalisation by offering wider seats aisle on some of its aircraft.
Without the support of the only other major manufacturer of modern big aircraft, experts say it is unlikely that its appeal to the aeronautical industry to put in place a waist-supplier standard, but it could obscure the 777X sales wave.
Oracle shareholders irritated by Ellison salaries
Oracle shareholders voted on Thursday against the compensation proposal for founder and managing director Larry Ellison, in view of his group's financial performance.
The vote is non-compulsory but tarnish the image of Larry Ellison, the world's third fortune just crowned with the victory of his boat at the America's Cup.
