The new anti-nicotine vaccine could suppress the pleasure of smoking
Scientists have developed an anti-nicotine vaccine that could suppress the pleasure of smoking a cigarette.
Only one dose of the vaccine was able to protect mice from nicotine dependence for life.
Further tests were needed before they started human trials, but Professor Ronald Crystal of Weill Cornell Medical College in New York stated that the early signs were encouraging.
“We really hope that this kind of vaccine strategy can help the millions of smokers who have tried to quitting, exhausting all the methods now available on the market, but discover that their addiction is strong enough to overcome these different approaches,” said Professor Cornell.
The new vaccine contains an inoffensive virus which has been modified to carry the genetic information necessary to design anti-nicotine antibodies.
Virus selectively infects cells in the liver, which then begins to produce a steady flow of antibodies.
Antifolcidies stalk all nicotine molecules present in the blood system and neutralise them before they reach the brain, which prevents the smoker from having its dose of nicotine.
During the tests, vaccinated mice which were subsequently given nicotine continued their activity normally.
Moulders that hadn't been vaccinated "were soothing," said the researchers, a sign that nicotine had reached their brains.
The experiments are described in the journal Science Translational Medicine.
Preceding tobacco vaccines have been shown to be ineffective as they contained antibodies.
Injections had to be repeated so frequently that antibody levels remained high that they were too expensive and impractical.
But the cost of the new vaccine should be much less because it transforms liver cells into antibody factories.
Professor Crystal has stated that if the future vaccine for human use was completely safe, it could be given to children before they were tempted to try a cigarette, which would prevent nicotine addiction.
But it is more likely to be used by smokers who want to stop smoking.
“They will know that if they start smoking again, they will not have any pleasure because of the nico-tine vaccine, and it can help them lose their bad habit,” he added.
British scientists stated that the results were interesting but pointed out that much further research was needed.
