A study aimed at multiplying the advantages of the HS2 railway project for Scotland was launched by the UK Government.
HS2 Ltd suggests that high-speed services to Scotland and the north of England will start as soon as Phase 1 opens in 2026.
Transport Minister Baroness Kramer has stated that the project "would reunite the United Kingdom".
The Scottish Minister for Transport, Keith Brown, was "exercised" about working with the British Government on the project.
Phase 1 will cover a new high-velocity railway line between London and the West Midlands.
Once Phase 2 is completed, the lines will serve Manchester and Leeds.
In June, the government increased the estimated cost of constructing the high-velocity link between London and the North of England from EUR 32.7 billion to EUR 42.
The United Kingdom Government in talks with Transport Scotland has asked HS2 Ltd to investigate the strengthening of railway capacity and improving journey times for northern England and Scotland.
This must include the possibility of travel times between Glasgow and Edinburgh of up to three hours.
Baroness Kramer said: “Our goal for HS2 relates to a truly national network that will reconcert the United Kingdom and its cities.”
We are pushing the HS2 project because its benefits are enormous.
Without it, we are faced with a problem of the capacity of our railway network.
But it is also about increasing connectivity between the 18 major UK cities, including Glasgow and Edinburgh, so that connections between them are better thanks to HS2.
British Secretary of State for Scotland Alistair Carmichael added: “Today’s announcement is good news for Scotland.”
For the Scottish Government, Keith Brown called on Mr Carmichael to “uneminently” support Scotland’s inclusion in the HS2 network.
Mr. Brown said: “The high-speed train has the potential to offer considerable economic benefits to Scotland, but it also adds Scotland's economic weight to the overall high-speed rail project throughout Great Britain.
“We are therefore excited to work in partnership with the UK Government to explore options for bringing the high-speed train network into Scotland, creating an advantage for all and complementing the Glasgow-Edinburgh route that the Scottish Government is already planning.
“I am looking forward to reading the review report with British ministers next year, and we will then jointly decide on the next steps.”
Rules relating to the use of on-board electronic devices remain in force in Australia for the time being
Australian air passengers will have to continue to turn off their tablets and smartphones during take-off and landing, despite the United States gesture aimed at relaxing the rules on electronic devices.
The U.S. Federal Aviation Administration left the door open to U.S. carriers to change their procedures so that passengers could read e-books, watch videos, or play gaming on their aircraft during critical flight phases provided they were in "airplane mode".
Passengers can already do so for most of the flight, but many people find it difficult not to access their e-books during take-off and landing.
Australian hauliers are examining the decision which obliges US carriers to carry out significant work in order to meet the requirements, but have indicated that they do not immediately intend to change their procedures.
The Civil Aviation Safety Authority also stated that it was reviewing the advertisement but pointed out that restrictions relating to the use of electronic devices during critical flight phases were still in force in Australia.
“CASA does not have any specific regulations governing the use of airborne electronic devices,” she said.
The issue is subject to regulation obliging aircraft operators to ensure the maintenance of safety at all times and passengers to comply with safety instructions given by crew members.
Virgin, who previously told CASA about its intention to expand the use of its on-board entertainment system using Wi-Fi technology, was open for change, but said it would follow the regulator.
“We would welcome a CASA revision that would allow the use of electronic devices because we truly believe that it would improve the customer experience now that we have (the on-flight on-board Wi-Fi entertainment system) on our airplanes,” said a spokesperson.
Qantas has stated that for the time being it complies with the current rules.
“According to our current policy, electronic devices cannot be used during take-off and landing, and we do not immediately intend to change that,” she said.
The FAA decision applies to U.S. airlines.
However, we are always interested in regulatory developments that could benefit passengers, and we will look closely at the FAA decision and its reasons.
For U.S. carriers, the impact of the decision will vary from one airline to another and will depend on the age of their fleet.
Carriers will need to demonstrate that their aircraft can tolerate radio interference from electronic devices, as well as reviewing manuals, training materials, hand luggage programmes and instructions provided to passengers.
“As soon as an airline has verified the tolerance of its fleet, it can allow passengers to use light portable electronic devices, such as tablets, e-book readers and smartphones at all altitudes,” the FAA said.
In the rare cases of low visibility, the crew will ask passengers to turn off their aircraft during landing.
The group also recommended that heavier aircraft be safely stowed under seats or upper compartments during take-off and landing.
