Tasmania Health-seller Rescue Plan of EUR 325
The Federal Government Maintains that the EUR 325 million bailout for the faltering health system is subject to strict conditions to ensure that the state government does not squander the funds.
Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek has announced that the Commonwealth is taking “urunciable measures” to put an end to a crisis caused by an aging island population, the high rate of chronic diseases, and system constraints.
The four-year funding was decided after government consultations with Tasmanian Independent MP Andrew Wilkie.
“The government has proposed an emergency rescue package which, in our view, will address the unique challenges facing the State,” Plibersek said today.
The EUR 325 million plan includes a campaign of EUR 31 million for non-emergency surgical operations.
An additional 2,600 operations, notably in the field of orthopaedic surgery and cataract, will help to catch up.
Provision is also being made for walk-and-one clinics in Hobart and Launceston, improved out-of-course care, the training of medical managers, mental health services and the deployment of electronic medical record systems in local hospitals.
“These investments are in line with the idea of front-line clinicians as the best way to treat Tasmania’s health care system,” Plibersek said.
The minister stated that the Tasmanian government would be faced with a strict reporting and accountability regime.
The Government will need to maintain the current levels of financing in order to receive Commonwealth money and prepare monthly reports on the sectors in which it spends additional funds.
A commission composed of three persons will be set up to ensure that the State provides the services in the most effective and efficient manner possible.
Mr. Wilkie has said today that 325 million euros will not count for much “until they are followed by genuine reform to consolidate Tasmania’s public health-care system”.
Nevertheless, he congratulated the Government for responding to its request for urgent assistance to the Prime Minister in early May.
“I hope that the federal aid plan will go a long way in removing Tasmania’s public health system from the blacklist,” Wilkie said.
According to the State Government, these additional non-emergency procedures will largely reverse the recent cuts.
But Opposition health spokesman Peter Dutton believes that today’s announcement is an “make-of-shift solution.”
“The reason we're here is because the state’s Labour government has withdrawn 430 million dollars from its health system,” he said on ABC TV.
You can't have a state government that wrests almost half a billion dollars and the Commonwealth puts 300 million dollars, pretending that it's a splendid day.
Mr. Dutton had visited Ms Plibersek to ensure that no dollar from the rescue package would be spent on additional bureaucracy.
Guillaume Nicloux's adaptation of Denis Diderot's novel boasts a remarkable artistic direction and meticulous genius, but it is also more laborious than it should be.
