Syria has destroyed its chemical weapons production capacity, says monitoring agency
Syria has destroyed essential equipment for the production of chemical weapons and munitions filled with toxic gases, the International Chemical Weapons Monitoring Body said on Thursday as violent clashes raged in the north of the country, close to one of the sites where toxic agents are supposed to be stockpiled.
A group of Syrian activists also said on Thursday that more than 120,000 people have been killed since the start of the civil war in the country almost three years ago.
Announcement by the Organization for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons (CWOP) arrived one day ahead of the cut-off date.
1 November established by the organization based in The The Hague ordered Damascus to destroy or “make unusable” all chemical-weapon production units and machinery for mixing toxic gases and filling ammunition.
Completing what is essentially the initial destruction phase is an important step in the ambitious timetable for the destruction of chemical weapons in Damascus by mid-2014.
Thanks to the destruction of equipment, Syria will no longer be able to produce new chemical weapons.
However, Damascus has yet to begin the destruction of existing weapons and reservations.
It is believed that there are approximately 1 000 garbifice of chemical products and weapons, including must-have gas and sarin neurotoxic agent.
Announcement came as the fighting raged in the city of Safira on Thursday, which, according to experts, hosts a chemical weapons production unit as well as storage sites, the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights based in Great Britain reported.
Activist group, which is following the changing human toll through a network of activists based in Syria, said on Thursday that 120,296 people had died.
It is estimated that 61,067 civilians, including 6,365 children.
On behalf of the government, an estimated 29,954 members of the armed forces of President Bashar al-Assad were killed, 18,678 of whom were pro-government fighters and 187 were Lebanese Hiz Hizbullah militants.
It is also estimated that 2,202 were deserters and some 5,375 opposition fighters, many of whom were foreigners.
On 25 July, the United Nations rated 100 per 1,000 deaths in the conflict since March 2011.
It has not updated this figure since then.
Some 2 million people had to flee the country as a result of the conflict.
Assad’s troops have been fighting the rebels, many of whom are linked to groups in the Al Qaeda network in Safira for weeks.
The Observatory stated on Thursday that there were losses on both sides, but gave no details.
The fighting has highlighted the importance of the dangers faced by chemical weapons inspectors when they must complete their mission of ridding Syria of its toxic arsenal in the midst of an ongoing civil-torn civil ulterior.
An IOAC statement, working closely with the United Nations, stated that its team was “now satisfied with having controlled – and seen destroyed – all reported critical production and machines for blending and recharging.”
It added that “no further inspection activities are planned at this time”.
Earlier in the week, the Inspectors stated that they had completed the first stage of the verification work, visiting 21 of the 23 sites reported by Damascus.
They were unable to visit the last two sites due to safety concerns, the inspectors said.
On Thursday, the OPCW stated that the two sites were, according to Syria, “abandoned and... that the chemical weapons programme elements they contained had been moved to other declared sites, which were inspected”.
It was undisturbed at first glimpse whether Safira's unit was one of the two sites that the OPCW inspectors were unable to visit.
Syria submitted a plan for the total destruction of its chemical weapons which was approved the following month by the Executive Committee of the OPCW.
“I salute the moral strength and the courage that you have all demonstrated in the most difficult mission ever undertaken by this organization,” said Ahmet Uzumcu’s Director-General in a commentary published by the OPCW.
Now in its third year, the civil war mainly opposes Sunni-Muslim Rebels to the Assad government and its security forces, who are supported by members of the Alawite sect, a branch of Shiite Islam.
On the other hand, the director of the Syrian Human Rights Observatory stated that there was a strong explosion on Wednesday at an air defence base in the Syrian coastal province of Latakia.
The cause of the blast remained unknown, he added.
Wrinkle culminating in the sentence imposed on one of Bali's Bombers
Survivivors and relatives of the 202 persons killed in the 2002 Bali attacks reacted angrily to the sentence imposed on the last conspirader to be brought to justice, saying that Umar Patek should be shot by an enforcement platoon.
Patek, who spent almost 10 years on the run by being one of the most sought-after men in South-East Asia, was sentenced yesterday to 20 years'imprisonment for its role in the manufacture of the explosive devices used in the attacks.
He could be discharged after 15 years on parole.
