FAA: Air passengers can now use gadgets aboard aircraft (but don’t make a call with their mobile phone)
Airlines passengers will be able to use their door-to-door e-mobilizers to read, work, play games, watch movies and listen to music – but not talk on their mobile phones – under the much-anticipated new guidelines published by the Federal Aviation Administration on Thursday.
But passengers should not expect immediate changes.
Speed with which the change will be put in place will vary from one airline to another, FAA administrator Michael Huerta said at a press conference.
Airlines will have to prove to the FAA that their aircraft comply with the new guidelines and that they have updated the Crew Trainee Training Manuals and the rules regarding the layout of aircraft in order to comply.
The FAA stated that it had already received plans from some airlines aimed at expanding the use of electronic devices on their aircraft.
Delta and JetBlue are among the companies that have already submitted plans.
“Depending on the plan, we could approve an extended use of electronic devices very soon,” the FAA said in a statement.
At the present time, passengers must turn off their smartphones, shelves, and other devices as soon as the aircraft doors are closed.
They don't supposed to turn them on again until the plane reaches an altitude of 10,000 feet and the captain gives its green light.
Custolds are expected to turn off their aircraft when the aircraft is lowered before landing and do not turn them on again until the plane has landed.
According to the new guidelines, airlines whose aircraft are properly protected from electronic interference will be able to allow passengers to use their aircraft during take-offs, landings and ground routing, the FAA said.
Most of the new airliners and other planes which have been modified so that passengers can use Wi-Fi at higher altitudes should meet the criteria.
Laura Glading, President of the Association of Professional Flight Attendants, was pleased with these changes.
“Until the new policy is safely implemented – and we will work closely with carriers to do so – it will benefit everyone,” Glading explained in a statement.
Frankly, we're really tired of feeling like "room supervisors" when it comes to this issue.
But it will always be forbidden to connect to the Internet to surf, exchange emails and text messages or upload data below 10,000 feet, the agency said.
Passengers will have to put their Smart Smart Smartphones, Tablets and other aircraft in aircraft mode.
So still No Words WithFriend, the Scrabble-style online game played by the actor Alec Baldwin on his smartphone in 2011 when he was rushed out of an American Airlines plane after refusing to turn off his device while the aircraft was parked at the gate.
And heavier devices like laptops should always be tidy because they could injure someone if they flew through the cabin.
Mobile phones on-board will also continue to be banned.
Statutory authority for mobile phones belongs to the Federal Communications Commission rather than the FAA.
FAA can lift the ban on certain electronic devices during take-off and landing
Over the past month, Mark Rosenker of the National Transportation Safety Board, a national transport safety expert on CBS News, stated that mobile phones were still considered to pose a risk.
“Mobilical phones are really a problem, not only because they could possibly create interference with navigational instruments, but because we know, according to the FCC, that they could disrupt mobile relay antennuities if used on board,” said Rosenker.
An industry advisory committee set up by FAA to examine the problem last month recommended that the government authorize a wider use of personal electronic devices.
The pressure on the FAA has increased in recent years to ease restrictions on their use.
Critics such as Senator Claire McCaskill, D-Mo., argue that there are no valid security reasons for these bans.
Restrictions are also becoming more and more difficult to apply because the use of devices has become ubiquitous.
Some studies indicate that at least a third of passengers forget to turn off their aircraft, or do so deliberately.
The FAA began to limit the use of electronic devices in 1996 as a result of reports of interference with navigation and communication instruments when passengers were carrying FM radio, the high-tech gadget at that time.
New airliners rely much more on electrical systems than previous generations of airplanes, but they are also designed to withstand electronic and FAA-approved interference.
The airlines allow their passengers to use Wi-Fi at cruising altitudes for several years.
Aircraft modified to accommodate Wi-Fi systems also have a better resistance to interference.
The vast majority of airliners are expected to permit wider use of electronic devices under the new guidelines, said Huerta.
Nowaday's electronic devices generally emit much less powerful radio transmissions than previous generations of devices.
Readers of e-books, for example, issue only minimal transmissions when running a page.
But the transmissions are stronger when the devices are downloading or sending data.
Amazon.com is one of those pressing for relaxation of restrictions on the use of electronic devices by passengers.
In 2011, the company's managers assassinated an airplane of Kindle e-book readers and flew it to see if there were any problems.
This has inspired members of the FAA Advisory Committee with mixed feelings as to the question of whether or not the use of devices is at risk.
Douglas Kidd of the National Association of Airline Passengers has stated that he believes interference from the aircraft is real, even if the risk is minimal.
Other members of the committee stated that there are only anecdotal reports from pilots that indicate that aircraft can interfere with the systems of an airplane, and that most of them are very old.
However, the Committee recommended that the FAA allow pilots to ask passengers to turn off their aircraft during instrument landings in low-visibility conditions.
A group in the travel industry welcomed the changes, calling them common-street arrangements for tech-enhavied travellers.
“We are pleased that the FAA recognises that a pleasant passenger experience is not incompatible with safety,” said Roger Dow, President and Director of the U.S. Travel Association.
