Is the European Eltor ready to do business with Great Britain?
The Business-for-Tridom campaign was launched in April with the promise to reunite businesses and to define what creators of wealth and jobs want a change in our relationship with the EU.
To do this, we ordered the largest and most comprehensive investigation from British business leaders and asked them what they were thinking about Great Britain, businesses and the EU.
YouGov questioned more than 1 000 business leaders, broadly representative of all company sizes, sectors and regions of Great Britain.
The conclusions of the survey will come as a surprise to many.
We have discovered that the vast majority of companies are now seeking to export outside of Europe, focusing on countries that are modernising and developing as EU countries stagnate.
They want the government to give priority to new trade links with countries such as China, India and Brazil rather than getting bogged down in the long and arduous process of reforming European institutional shrikes.
When asked for their views on specific policy issues – ranging from croopolis regulation to product laws – the majority of business leaders believe that the monitoring of these key competences should return to Westminster.
The Single Market caused widespread discontaneity, with companies saying that the cost of the Brussels settlement is disproportionate to the benefits of being part of the European trading area – 40% of large companies, traditionally the most pro-European, are even in agreement.
Last but not least, our survey of business leaders revealed that a large majority wanted Great Britain to pursue initiatives to change the Treaty and to develop a relationship with the EU based on trade and non-politics.
This conclusion, which ends up in all major groups and companies of all sizes, shows that the business world insists on a “significant change” that restores power in the United Kingdom.
What is at stake is high – succeeding in changing the treaty, and better representing Britain’s interests, it would result in 16% of the vote in favor of maintaining the country in the EU in a referendum.
The Prime Minister should have no doubt for a moment: this investigation shows that British companies are supporting this plan to renegotiate the terms of Britain’s accession to the EU.
It also shows that the companies expect renegotiating as a significant change in the current balance of power in favour of returning to the hands of the United Kingdom.
It is increasingly necessary to better represent the interests of United Kingdom companies, since the euro area is embarking on the path of a closer economic and fiscal union.
The priority must be jobs and growth in Great Britain and, as the conclusions of our survey show, for businesses this means re-emphasising trade and a fundamental change in the Brussels regulatory approach.
