Alexei Miller de Gazprom says Bulgarian gas pipeline inaugurates a new gas era
The start of the construction of the South Stream gas pipeline in Bulgaria marks the launch of one of the largest energy projects in Europe, Gazprom said.
'Historical event has taken place today: Construction began in the Bulgarian part of the South Stream gas Pipeline, Europe's largest large-scale project,' Alexei Miller, Managing Director of Gazprom, said on Thursday.
This project is a key element of energy security throughout the European continent.
South Stream is expected to be used to diversify the export routes from Russia to Europe.
A contractual dispute between Gazprom and its counterpart from Ukraine, the country through which the largest proportion of Russian gas destined for Europe passes, increases the level of risk compared to conventional routes, adds the director.
Miller added that direct junction towards Bulgaria, a member of the European Union, means that the geopolitical risks associated with transit countries are eliminated “forever”.
Bulgarian consumers will receive South Stream gas at a reduced rate as soon as the entire project is put into operation in 2015.
Gazprom indicated that construction is expected to commence in other downstream countries by the end of the year.
The gas pipeline was designed for an annual capacity of 63 billion cubic meters of natural gas.
Pitcairn Wants to Create the Largest Marine Reservation in the World
The island of Pitcairn wants to establish the largest marine reserve in the world, the Tahiti News newspaper announced on Thursday, based on Radio Australia.
This island, to the eastern part of the Gambier Archipelago (French Poynesia), is the last British territory in the South Pacific, halfway between New zealand and Chile.
This territory measures 47 km2, if there are three other nearby small islands.
It has been British in 1790 by Bounty mutineers, an episode in the history of the English navy giving rise to three Hollywood adaptations.
A few dozen inhabitants, the majority of the descendants of Bounty mutineers, still live in Pitcairn, and 95 per cent of their income depends on the generosity of London.
The Island Council voted unanimously in favour of the creation of a marine reserve around the small archipelago, i.e. 836.000 km2 which corresponds to its exclusive economic zone.
A deputy mayor of the island went to London asking Britain to validate this request.
Mike Warren, the mayor of Pitcairn, believes that the creation of such a protected area would be an initial step towards greater financial self-governance for his island.
The project is to be accepted by the English Governor of Pitcairn, residing in New zealand, and by the Government of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.
The creation of such a marine reserve would enable the United Kingdom to sanctify this 836,000 km2, provided, however, that it has the means to ensure its supervision, which is not the case.
There is no airport on Pitcairn, which makes it impossible to base one or more aircraft dedicated to the monitoring of the area, and there is no port to place military surveillance vessels.
As for France, for its part, it already has to carry out the very costly aerial and maritime surveillance of the five-million-km2 EEE of French Polynesia, a neighbour of Pitcairn.
