Holiday Prison for Former Chinese Vice-Governor
A former vice-governor from Jilin province in north-eastern China was sentenced on Friday to life imprisonment for corruption.
Excluded from the Communist Party in July 2012, Tian Xueren was accused of receiving more than 19 million bribe yuan, the official Chinese media reported.
Between 1995 and 2001, the Vice-Governor, who was also the president of the Bank of Jilin, a public institution, helped businesses and managers obtain contracts, loans or promotions in exchange for money or gifts, said the first middle court in Beijing on its microblog.
President Xi Jinping, who took office last March, had made the fight against corruption a national priority, believing that the phenomenon posed a threat to the very existence of the Communist Party.
While the Head of the State promised that justice would be as intractable with the powerful “tigares” as with “feels” – subordinate civil servants – only a handgmine of high-ranking officials have been convicted, including former executives of the oil giant Petrochina.
The most dramatic recent case has been former CCP Chief in Chongqing Bo Xilai, sentenced to life in September for corruption and impovereed power after targeting the highest positions in the state.
However, the Government had made no intention to reform the anti-corruption system by, for example, establishing an independent party body.
Two YMCA employees charged with sexual assault following allegations made against Jonathan Lord at a hearing of the Royal Commission.
Two New South Wales YMCA employees were charged with sexual assault against children following allegations made by Caringbah Educator Jonathan Lord in 2011 during a hearing of the Royal Commission on Child Sexual Abuse.
But in its opening statement before the Commission, the YMCA stated that it “did never have to deal with a problem of sexual assault on children within its organization”.
Director-General Phillip Hare was questioned about two cases: a YMCA employee was charged with offences relating to child pornography and a physical education instructor at YMCA Caringbah Hall was found guilty of sexual abuse of children at the centre where he worked in 1991.
Mr. Hare told Gail Furness, an adviser to the Commission, that he had been aware of the first case but was not aware of the second case.
He also admitted that the YMCA opening declaration was inaccurate by pointing out that “YMCA external controls were carried out and indicated that child safety is a top priority for the organization.”
It was demonstrated to the Commission that the YMCA had been informed that it had received the worst second ratings on four institutions at a quality audit of the Department of Education and Communities carried out in August this year.
Mr. Hare, who began working at the YMCA at the age of 21, admitted that staff management had been a failure with Lord's recruitment and had failed to verify that the employees knew they were obliged to report any violations of children's safety policy.
Earlier this year, Lord was convicted of sexual assault on twelve boys during the two years he worked at the YMCA.
He was sentenced to a minimum of 6 years'imprisonment.
But Mr. Hare rejected the idea that the YMCA would have a cultural problem that prevented staff from reporting Lord's breaches of child safety.
The staff members testified that they had observed certain shortcomings, in particular the fact that Lord stayed lonely with the children, that he kept them in private, made them sit on his lap, told them that he loved them and let them play with his mobile phone.
Danielle Ockwell, who was mentored by lorry and had requested training in the protection of children because she was concerned about her behaviour, indicated that she found YMCA Caringbah's Child Services Officer Jacqui Barnat, who targeted Lord "very intimidating and difficult to approach most of the time".
The Director-in-Chief stated that he was dismissing the testimonies of the staff that they were reluctant to report such misconduct to their supervisor.
Instead, he stated that their friendly relations with lord blunted their judgement, which meant that they did not report its actions.
Mr. Hare stated that he had expressed his views before the New-Southern YMCA council that, for the organization, the lesson of the “Jonathan Lord Incident” should not relate to “reporting” by staff, and the council agreed with him.
Mr. Hare had said that the decision to get staff to sign confidentiality agreements, shortly after the allegations had been raised, had been taken by YMCA Director-in-Chief, Liam Whitley.
It stated that this decision was intended to avoid any contamination of the evidence, but that it was an “excess zeal” and had been ill-implified.
YMCA in New South Wales was not a child-secure organization when Jonathan Lord was employed there between 2009 and 2011, the expert on sexual abuse of children, Professor Stephen Smallbone of Griffith University, told the Commission.
He stated that there were “serious problems” in the recruitment, monitoring, initiation, training and supervision of staff.
The hearing was adjourned until 20 December.
