Discovery of a Drug Tunnel between the United States and Mexico with its own railway
One of the most sophisticated tunnels for drug trafficking between the United States of America and Mexico, with its own lighting, ventilation and electric rail systems, has been undisturbed.
U.S. authorities have described the four-by-three-strengthent tunnel as one of the most sophisticated no-sophisticated passes they had ever seen.
The zigzagant tunnel, equivalent to nearly six long soccer fields, connects warehouses near Tijuana, Mexico, to San Diego in the United States.
The area is replenaminated with indefinable warehouses, making it possible to easily hide trucks loaded with drugs.
The tunnel had been closed before the drugs came unnoticed, the authorities said.
The authorities seized 8.5 tons of marijuana and 148 kg of cocaine after the discovery of the tunnel, according to court records.
Three men who, according to the authorities, worked as drivers, were charged with possession of marijuana and cocaine with sales intent.
They are liable to imprisonment for a term ranging from 10 years to life imprisonment if convicted.
Smuggled smugglers in Nogales operate vast underground drainage channels.
The tunnel is the eighth major passage discovered in San Diego since 2006.
Some of the largest tunnels were uncovered following the marijuana harvest in central Mexico in October, posing a challenge for drug cartels, which must quickly deliver their products to consumers.
In 2010, the authorities found a passage of about 700 yards equipped with railway tracks from the kitchen of a Tijuana house and went up to two warehouses in San Diego.
United Kingdom police issue an extradition warrant against Assange
The British police today issued an extradition warrant against WikiLeak founder Julian Assange, who took refuge at the Embassy of Equateur in London and applied for political asylum.
Scotland Yard stated that it had issued a “remission notice” against the 40-year-old Australian, asking him to go to a police station, adding that if it did not do so, he would be likely to be arrested.
Assange could be extradited in Sweden following allegations of sexual crimes, after exhausting all possible channels under British law after the Supreme Court invalidated his appeal against his extradition at the beginning of the month.
Feeling that Stockholm would authorize its transfer to the United States, it took refuge at the Embassy of Ecuador in London on 19 June, demanding political asylum from South America.
Scotland Yard has "issued a delivery notice against a 40-year-old man who asks him to come to a police station on the date and time of his choice," a spokeswoman said.
It shall continue to violate the conditions for his release on bail.
The Embassy declined to comment on the issuing of the notice by the police.
Assange fears being extradited from Sweden to the United States where he could be accused of espionage after unveiling more than 250,000 US diplomatic cables on the WikiLeaks website battling secret information.
How Biometrics Will Involve Our Lives
Girl the passwords to type to access your smartphone or pay for your purchases?
U.S. (Freamer and Japanese) researchers are predicting a future where we will be recognized by the biometric sensors of phones and computers.
Bernard Didier, Deputy Director-in-Chief of Morpho, even believes that "the century will be biometric".
Only biometrics will make it possible to ascertain the identity of a person making transactions in such a transverse and cross-transnational universe as the Internet.
Privacy-related organisations are confirming this infatuation with biometrics, but are concerned about it.
In 2011 alone, the National Commission for Informatics and Freedoms authorised 774 systems for recognizing imprints, the shape of the hand or the venous hand network in companies, administrations, canteens, etc.
'Everyone can soon be identified, anywhere and at any any moment,' said Justin Brookman, Consumer Privacy Manager at CDT (Center for Democracy and Technology) in D.T.
The reasons for such an optimism, among some, and pessimism among others?
Biometrics will be based on new techniques, which will make it possible to provide new services for citizens and consumers.
Nowadays, in France, the most widely used biometric data are the fingerprints, the contour of the hand and the venous network of the palm or the finger.
But each of these techniques possesses its constraints.
'For example, some people who are working on concrete have very spoiled fingers and their imprints are illegible,' says Philippe Robin, Technical Director of the Identity Domain at Thales Communications and Security.
And the digest as well as the verification of the venous network and the contour of the hand involves a voluntary and precise gesture of people.
It is at the same time a security - we cannot capture these data without their knowledge - and a drawback: the procedure sometimes has to be repeated and therefore takes time.
As a result, research has been being carried out some 20 years on other approaches, such as facial or iris recognition (the coloured part of the Eye).
By increasing sensor accuracy and the computing capacity of the computers needed for data analyzing, these techniques are becoming operational.
Precision facial recognition increased ten-fold over the last five years, Cyrille Bataller, Director of Acccenture's Research and Development Laboratories in Europe.
With our help, the United Kingdom and the Netherlands have deployed automatic border crossings using facial recognition.
It is now possible to identify a moving face or iris.
'Today we're researching voice recognition or how to walk using sound sensors, but we need a quiet environment,' said Sridhar Iyengar, Director of Security Research at the Intel Labs.
A unique and falsifiable marker, DNA also raises hopes and worries.
'At the current state of knowledge, it can be regarded as the ultimate biometric data,' confirms Sophie Vulliet-Tavernier, Director of Studies, Innovation and Foresight at the CNIL.
But DNA analysis is still very long and very expensive.
NEC offers judicial law enforcement a suitcase costing EUR 90 000 for analyzing samples of DNA at the scene of a crime within an hour.
The uses of biometrics are divided into two groups: identification (recognizing one person among others) and authentication (ensuring that the person is the one he or she pretends to be).
Until now, the identification concerned the issuing of identity papers: it is necessary to compare biometric elements (napsets, photographs, iris) with the information that the State possesses about all the citizens already registered.
This makes it possible to make sure that a person does not attempt to amuse the identity of someone else.
This principle of comparison may have other applications.
NEC thus offers the recognition of VIP at the entrance to a hotel or shop.
'We are retrieving images from the surveillance cameras and comparing them to the photos of celebrities circulating freely on the Internet,' explains Dany Nassif, practicing commercial development at NEC France.
Another use is to take a picture of a quidam queuing in a store, follow its progression through facial recognition and calculate the waiting time.
Authentication initially involved physical (borders, protected premises, canteens . . .) or logical (opening of a computer).
More recently, presence control has been added.
'A biometric pointer avoids the phenomenon of the buddy who points for another,' justifies Cyril Bataller from Accenture.
Increasingly however, authentication will also concern transactions, in particular those carried out from devices connected to the Internet.
In Japan, it is already possible to withdraw money from certain cash dispensers by inserting his card and placing his hand on a biometric reader: this gesture replaces the typing of the secret code.
A similar technique is tested in Villeneuve d'Ascq and Angoulême by the company Natural Security in partnership with banks and large-distribution signs: when paying in-stores with its bank card, the customer does not type in its code, but introduces its finger into a reader that scans its veins.
The experimentation is scheduled to last six months.
Biometric readers could soon reach French merchants.
Ways to reassuring users
There are three ways of making biometric seduced in the eyes of the general public.
The first is to explain that it can save time.
'While, thanks to biometrics, a customer stays in check-out for 30 seconds, it's appreciable,' explains the manager of a large area.
The second is to offer personalised services: in a matter of a few years, a vocal server will be able to recognise your voice and propose tailor-made options.
Finally, reassuring by highlighting the precautions taken to protect the databases.
'Freckprints are stored on a first server; people's identities are in a second base: the correlation between the two numbers is encrypted and stored in a highly secure box, which locks if one tries to dissipate it,' says Philippe Robin of Thales.
But, for lack of in-depth studies, it is impossible to know whether these speeches really convince the users.
Privacy specialists continue to be a matter of concern.
'Progress in facial recognition, the proliferation of surveillance cameras and the huge amount of photos available on Facebook, Flickr or Picasa make me fear the worst: widespread surveillance,' predicts Andrew Patrick of the Privacy Commissioner in Canada.
