Scientists have come to shed light on how the movements of a dog tail are related to its mood.
Previous studies had revealed that happy dogs swirled their tails further to the right (from the dog's point of view), while nervous dogs shook it further to the left.
But from now on, scientists say that dogs can detect these subtle differences and respond to them.
Prof. Georgio Vallortigara, a neuroscientist at the University of Trete, said: "Everyone knows that in humans, the left and right-hand sides of the brain are involved differently in stimuli that arouse positive or negative emotions."
Here, we've been trying to figure out what it is in other species.
He added that, just as in humans, the right-hand side of the brain in dogs was responsible for movement to the Left and vice versa, and that both hemispheres played a different role in e emotions.
Learn more about how dogs are reacting to messy tail movements of other dogs, the researchers tracked animals looking at films featuring other dogs.
They measured animals' heart rate and analysed their behaviour.
Surely it won't be long before we understand why their tail sometimes goes on one side and sometimes on the other.
Prof. Vallortigara explained: “We presented to dogs films featuring other dogs – either a naturalistic version or a silhouette to avoid any other confounding factors – and we were able to trajectinate the tail movement and present it as going further to the left or to the right.”
When the animals saw an unexpressible dog moving its tail to the right (from the point of view of the shaft-watching dog), they stayed perfectly relaxed.
But when they spotted a tail deflected mainly to the left (again from the point of view of the shaggling dog), their heart rate picked up and they looked anxious.
Professor Vallortigara stated that he did not believe that the dogs deliberately communicated with each other through these movements.
Instead, it thinks that dogs know from experience the movements that they should or should not worry about.
He added: “If you meet other dogs several times and if you notice that, most of the time, the movement from their tail to one side is combined with a more friendly attitude and the right side leads to a less friendly attitude, you will react based on this experience.”
The researchers indicate that the results could enable owners, veterinarians and trainers to gain a better understanding of the e emotions of their animals.
Canine Behavioural Expert John Bradshaw, a visiting professor at Bristol's School of Veterinary Sciences, clarified that this was not the first study to examine the importance of the left and right in the canine species.
Over the past year, a Lincoln University team discovered that the dogs were turning their head to the left when they were looking at an aggressive dog and to the right when it was a cheerful dog.
And in another scientific study conducted by the University of Victoria in Canada, he said, “Dogs went more easily towards a robotic dog when its tail shackled to the left rather than to the right, instead of becoming anxious – reminiscent of what appears in the Italian study.”
He pointed out that the differences could come from the fact that the dogs that participated in the various studies did not fully consider the animals of films or robotic dogs such as dogs.
A study on how dogs react to real dogs might be useful, he explained.
“Although a lot of evidence in different mammal mammals indicate that both sides of the brain are used for different purposes, a lot of detail still needs to be clarified – and dogs are no exception,” he said.
However, given the ease with which their behaviour can be recorded, it will surely not be long before we understand why their tail sometimes moves on one side and sometimes on the other.
