Pope Francis Appoint First Cardinals in February
Pope Francis will appoint new cardinals of the Catholic Church for the first time on 22 February, the Vatican announced on Thursday.
The cardinals are the tallest clergy in the Catholic Church behind the Pope, and those who elucidate, so Francis will appoint his first group of men who will ultimately choose his successor.
There are currently 201 cardinals.
However, as soon as a cardinal reaches the age of 80, he can no longer participate in electing a pope – so this belongs to a group of 120 “cardinal electors.”
In a statement announcing the news, Father Federico Lombardi, a Vatican Spokesman, said that a meeting of all the existing cardinals would take place prior to the ceremonies in which bishops would be elevated to the dignity of cardinal, known as consistory.
“Pope Pope Francis decided to communicate his decision to summon the February consistory in advance in order to facilitate the planning of other meetings requiring the participation of cardinals from different parts of the world,” said Lombardi.
Jack Valero des Voix catholiques has stated that by February the number of cardinal electors will probably have fallen.
He stated that a Pope usually appointed as cardinals as necessary for the number of cardinal electors to reach 120 cardinals over the age of 80.
The consistory of next year will be important because it will be the first since Pope Francis’s election in March this year, Valero said.
For the time being, there is a kind of tendency to favour Europe, in particular Italy.
“It will be interesting to see whether the new Pope will appoint cardinals from the rest of the world to restore the balance,” he added.
40 per cent of Roman Catholics live in South America, but a small number of cardinals represent them.
The cardinals would also be the first to be chosen since Francis shaped the Cardinal Council, a group of eight cardinals from all over the world to examine the means of reforming the church.
Previously, the Pope decided by himself alone.
“François has now chosen these eight cardinals to help him,” Valero explained.
He added that it was "completely possible" that Francis would seek advice from cardinals.
But we’ve never experienced such a situation – it’s completely new.
Valero stated that poskins generally raised bishops representing vast communities as cardinal, but that Francis was "quiet of surprises – so we don't know who he would name."
