Abama's Elector of Despair
I voted twice for President Obama, believing in the possibility of a change
He Says Obama Did Commemorated Filamented by Republican Party Obstructionism
Obstructionism doesn't overeample the Obamacare Center's Dirone Attacks or Drone Attack
The memoir of Obama's 2008 campaign is a sad remembrance of what could have happened
Nathaniel P. Morris is a second-year medical student at the Harvard School of Medicine.
I'm reading a terribly sad book these days.
It's a book that, I thought, would cheer me during the glimpsehood of the second year of medicine and give me hope.
It is called "The Audacity to Win," and it is the memoirs of Barack Obama’s 2008 presidential campaign.
When I've finished the medical reports of my patients in the evening and I go to bed, this book takes me back to a time when politicians were inspiring millions of people and speeches could breathe your breath.
The election ended with a landslifting victor, and Newspaper presenters took the time to reflect on the historical nature of the moment.
My comracious comrades cried with rejoice and my parents kept all the newspapers they found.
A young team of visionaries was moving towards the White House, and the nation was ready for change.
During the transition period of Obama before taking office in 2008, 82 per cent of favourable views.
And then I close the book.
Getting back to the present is a difficult waermaker, it's like coming out of a dream.
It's hard to remember those days full of fantasy, it's like distant memories, a sad reminiscent of the opportunities of the past.
The change really took place in the years that followed the first time I voted.
It didn't have anything to do with what I imagined.
Appropriating Obama with great achievements, from the Affordable Care Act (Affordable Care Act) to the departing from Iraq of our army, to the end of the “no seen or known” and the assassination of Osama bin Laden.
In addition, I believe that partisan obstructionism has unleashed too many efforts aimed at advancing our nation in the field of immigration reforms, public health care and the closure of Guantanamo, among other things.
But after repeatedly defending Obama’s administration in the face of my peers and my colleagues, I can’t provide any explanation.
I am on the brink of political desperation.
Republican obstruction cannot explain the tweeting of foreign rulers or the attacks of innocent foreign children through drones.
It could not explain that the National Security Agency was collecting data on the privacy of Americans, or the implicating whistleblowers revealing government misdemeanours.
It cannot justify the assassination of Anwar al-Awlaki, an American citizen, without a lawsuit or the evasion of public funding and overshooting authorized spending during the presidential campaigns.
It cannot justify the conclusions of a report which says that the White House's efforts to silence the media are the "most aggressive ... since Nixon's administration".
And, more recently, it cannot excuse the impossibility to design a simple website for more than three years after the Affordable Care Act was ratified.
I don't know if that's what I expected.
If, at the age of 18, I was supposed to understand that governance could contradict the political campaigns that predated it.
Of course, the electoral function is unforeseeable, because the Opposition Political Party and the events that occur by chance, such as the Newton Massachusale, influence our public conversation.
However, all the examples that I have listed above seem to be largely the choice of administration.
That's what bothers me the most.
I voted again for Obama in 2012, but not because I was enthusiastic about his candidacy.
Mitt Romney presented a conflicted and unparalleled alternative to determine his policies or positions.
I felt that Obama, elected for a second-time term with the pressure of future elections, would keep the promises that had been said about for so long.
Yet, at a time when the favourable opinion index plugged into 45 per cent this week, it is even more difficult to go back in 2008 through this book.
It makes me desire the many promises that have evaporated.
This week, I read the part of the book that describes how Obama suffered huge losses to Clinton at the Pennsylvanian Primaries.
At a post-campaign meeting, he told his team that they had to get back on the right track and remain faithful to their cause.
“I want us to revolt on our talent,” he said.
We need to remember who we are.
Five years later, Mr President, and I no longer agree with you.
The views expressed in this commentary are only those of Nathaniel Morris.
