A team of biologists led by the Wildlife Conservation Society in New York has identified a new hump dolphin species off the northern coast of Australia.
Thibaut Bouveroux, Scientist at the Circumstance for the Preservation and Study of Marine Animals and Milieux (OCEAMM) returns to L'Express for this discovery and explains the long process of identification of new species.
Is the discovery of this new kind of hump dolpha astonishing you?
This is good tidings, but I am not particularly surprised.
If in the past species have been described and identified on a morphological, anatomical and geographical basis, today, the development of new tools such as genetics allows us to improve our scientific knowledge of species classification.
Moreover, it is not impossible that these advances in genetics and molecular biology will call into question the classification or belonging of a species to a genus, a family or even an order.
At the beginning of the 19th century, morphologists categorized animals, based on morphological differences between species.
Nowadays, some of these rankings are called into question due to advances in genetics.
In the same way, two phmorphologically very similar species were distinguished thanks to genetics.
This is the case of the new species that has just been identified.
About a decade ago, the scientific community recognized two species belonging to this subfamility: Pacific hump dolphins and hump whales of the Atlantic.
Recent analyses made it possible to distinguish between four species.
Why is this an important discovery, as the Wildlife Conservation Society argued?
From a conservation point of view, it is essential to know these genetic differences, in order to protect the species and therefore its genetic variability.
Although this population had been known for a long time by the researchers, they did not know that it was part of a new species, present only there.
Therefore, if a problem of mass mortality caused by a viral or incidental capture epidemic arises in the region, we could face the ignition of a species, rather than the local disappearance of a population of a more widespread species on Earth.
This loss of genetic heritage would be much more problematic.
One species is considered threatened or erring depending on the number of individuals existing on the Earth, i.e. the stock, and its ability to recover from neighbouring stocks.
With regard to Sousa chinensis, from which the population of the new undisturbed species originated, its stock automatically became lower, thus making it more vulnerable.
Why is it rare to uncover new marine mammal species?
Marine mammals are located at the top of the food chain.
They live in homogenous, open habitats, in which there are numerous genetic exchanges between populations and individuals.
This brewing of genes limits the creation of new species.
On the other hand, more enclosed ecosystems promote geneolating genetic isolation, which can eventually lead to the creation of new species.
It must be noted that the marine environment is the least well-known environment.
We'd rather send robots to Mars than to know what's living in the Mariana Tree, just 11 kilometers from the surface of the sea.
We spend more money looking for life on the other planets than in marine research, and there's a lot of time it changed.
RBS Susposes Two Foreign Exchange Traders
Royal Bank-of-Spois has suspended two traders from its foreign-exchange-market division according to two similar sources, which shows once again that the global regulator's investigation into suspected manipulation of the foreign-currency market is beginning to bear fruit.
Some of the largest banks in the world, including UBS, Barclays, Deutsche Bank, and RBS, have confirmed that they are cooperating with regulators as part of the world’s largest financial market investigations, where USD 5.3 trillion change hands every day.
The two traders were the first RBS employees to be pilled as part of the expanded investigation, which echoed the Libor Inter-Banking Distribution.
The bank, which refused to comment on the suspensions, confirmed earlier this month having received requests for information from the regulators.
“The ongoing investigation into this case is continuing and we are cooperating fully with FCA and other regulators,” said a bank representative two weeks ago.
Last month, sources close to the file stated that RBS had transmitted e-mail bribes and snapshot messages to and by a former trader to the UK regulator, the Financial Conduct Authority.
This trader, Richard Usher, left RBS in 2010 and would have been suspended from his position as Europe's Head of Currency Currency Trading at JPMorgan.
Rohan Ramchandani, Citi's European spot-exercise foreign-exercise manager, went on leave this week, while Matt Gardiner, a senior currency trader at Barclays and UBS, was suspended by Standard Chartered this week.
None of these traders has been charged.
Usher’s file included snapshot messages sent to and by bankers working at Barclays and Citigroup, added sources close to the folder.
UBS explained this week that it filed an action against some of its employees after the Swiss regulator, Finma, has stated that it is investigating suspected foreign-extinguish market manipulation in a number of Swiss banks.
In the world, at least six authorities – the European Commission, Finma, the Swiss Competition Authority Weko, FCA, the U.S. Department of Justice and the Hong Kong Currency Utility – are investigating allegations about bankers who have agreed to manipulate foreign-exchange-market rates.
HSBC, Citigroup, JPMorgan and Credit Suisse have also launched internal investigations or received requests for information from regulators, sources close to the case reported.
Banks spawn snapshot instant messages and e-mails sent for years to find cases of wrongdoing.
Circumstances have shattered traders in a sector that has been one of the largest growth drivers of investment bank trading units in recent years, but has been questioned this year due to low currency volatility that limits opportunities for speculators.
Some bankers have tried to minimize the matter by saying it is almost impossible to manipulate the vast highly liquid foreign-exchange market, but senior traders explain that this isn't necessarily true.
A senior trader stated that, despite the high volume of day-to-day foreign-exchange transactions, the fragmentation of liquidity between trade management rigs and the increasingly frequent use by banks of their own in-factual pulses “explains that it is possible to start having an impact on the low-expensive market.”
The news arrived on the same day that Credit Suisse had announced that it had dismissed a trader working in its fund-to-fund office from the London Stock Exchange this week after it caused a loss of EUR 6 million at the end of last year.
The bank quickly informed the competent authorities and cooperated with the regulators.
“We are convinced that the trader acted alone and that the case was brought under control,” said a representative of Credit Suisse.
