Walks in polka diaphragm, jungle and crosses rivers to offer free medical assistance
Dr. Georges Bwelle provides free health-care in villages of Cameroon
Dr. Bwelle and his team spend almost all their weekends seeing hundreds of patients
There aren't a lot of doctors in this West-Canadian country, just 1 per 5,000 people.
Vote here or via your mobile device
Dr. Georges Bwelle is one of Ten CNN Heroes of 2013.
You can vote for him, or one of the other 10 heroes, to elect "CNN Hero of the Year."
This person will receive EUR 250,000 to continue his extraordinary work.
For 21 years, Georges Bwelle watched his sick father alternating periods of consciousness and recklessness and going to hospitals that were not equipped to help him.
Jamef Bwelle was injured in a car accident in 1981 near Yaounde, the capital of Cameroon.
At the beginning, he had only one broken arm, but he contracted an infection that propelled to his brain, creating a bruise whose sequelae he was going to carry the sequelae for the remainder of his life.
“There were no neurosurgians in Cameroon,” said Georges Bwelle.
We would have taken it abroad if we had had enough money.
Instead, Dr. Bwelle spent years accompaniing his father in overcrowded clinics and hospitable hospitals, getting any treatment he could receive.
“It’s not easy,” added Bwelle.
You can leave the house at 5 o'clock, rush to the hospital to be the first, and you're not the first.
There are a lot of patients.
People can die because they wait too long.
The situation did not change much since Dr. Bwelle's father died in 2002.
In Cameroon, there is only 1 doctor per 5,000 inhabitants, according to the World Health Organization.
By way of comparison, the ratio in the United States is one doctor per 413 inhabitants.
And even if they could see a doctor, many Cameroonians wouldn’t be able to pay for the consultation.
Two out of every five people in the country live below the poverty line, and almost three-quarters of the country’s health spending is spent in the private sector.
“The only problem they have is poverty,” explained Dr Bwelle.
And because of poverty, they can't enjoy life.
After having seen his dad and so many of his compatriots suffering, Dr. Bwelle was determined to try to change the course of things.
Dr. Georges Bwelle and his team of volunteers practicing more than 700 free surgical procedures over the past year over the past year.
He became a doctor himself, working as a vascular surgeon at the Central Hospital in Yaoundé.
And it created a non-profit humanitarian association, ASCOVIME, which travels to rural areas on weekends to provide free health care.
Since 2008, he and his group of volunteers have assisted nearly 32,000 people.
Almost every Friday, with about 30 people clapping in vans after attaching medical equipment to the roof and crossing difficult terrain to the villages most in need of assistance.
Luck doesn't always follow them.
They repeatedly had to push the vehicles to cross rivers or muddy tracks.
But when they arrive, they are received as a hero: the community organizes a party, sing and da da dancing and offers them the best available accommodation.
In these villages, free health-care is a good reason to celebrate, and Dr. Bwelle – with his big smile and overwhelming energy – is more than happy to join festivities.
By the next morning, the team began receiving hundreds of patients.
“We receive 500 people every visit,” Bwelle said.
They sometimes live 60 kilometers from the village, and they come on foot.
In this itinerant hospital, a variety of health-insecure health-insecure care.
Many people receive treatment against malaria, tuberculosis, malnutrition, diabetes, pests and sexually transmitted diseases.
Others may receive crutches, a pair of glasses collected, or a free birth certificate – document necessary to go to school, but many poor families simply cannot afford to buy it.
In the evening, the team perform simple surgical procedures under local anaesthetic.
Operations are usually carried out in a school, town hall or house; after the intervention, the patient gets up and goes to the recovery room to give his place to the next person.
Through generator lighting, the operating room and sterilization of the equipment, Dr. Bwelle and his volunteers work until the early hours of Sunday morning.
It is an horrendous rhythm, but village musicians generally help the team to stay motivated.
“They knock on drums all the night to keep us awake so that we can keep working,” Bwelle explained.
On Sunday, the team goes back to the city, tired but proud of the work it has done.
The group, composed of Cameroonian doctors and medical students, practiced 700 surgical procedures free of charge during the last year, and it knows that their presence can make all the difference to those to whom they come to the aid.
One man explained that the free operation he had undergone to treat a hernia would enable him to work again.
“It’s going to change my future and that of my family,” the man said.
In addition to supporting this itinerary hospital and working as a surgeon at the hospital, Dr. Bwelle also works for the night in private medical offices around Yaoundé.
It is this second job, he explains, for 60 per cent of its association; the rest is covered by private donations.
"I don't know when he's sleeping," said Katie O'Malley, a second-year medical student from Drexel University in Philadelphia and a volunteer in Dr. Bwelle's group.
He is still in the hospite or trying to find money for his association in order to be able to carry out these campaigns.
For medical students and nursing students like Katie O'Malley from the United States and from Europe to join Mr. Bwelle in his missions, this is an occasion for learning in the field that they don't have at home.
'We have been able to participate in operations to help blow up the blood or pass the instruments to Dr. Bwelle,' explained Miss O'Malley.
This isn't something that you have an opportunity to do in America when you're a second-year medical student.
Student volunteers usually pay for their trips to Cameroon, and often arrive with collected medical equipment.
But once in Yaounde, their pension, transportation and education are covered by Dr. Bwelle.
"He's a hero, there's no doubt," said Katie O'Malley.
He gave his life for this association, and his desire to help the Cameroonian people was not about to go extinct.
For Dr Bwelle, the relatively constant workload is not a difficulty.
Helping others live happily by keeping the promise he made to his father is something that gives him immense joy.
“I’m so happily when I do this job,” Bwelle added.
And I'm thinking about my dad.
I hope he sees what I'm doing.
Bringing people and relieving the pain are the reasons that drives me to do this.
Visit ASCOVIME to see how you can help.
