For example, I am wondering about the definition of rescisibility.
Above all, I am fighting, together with others, for the recognition of the legal status of the hostage.
In my opinion, there is a problem of semantics.
It is necessary to describe the taking of hostage-taking "policies" - which is the case for most of the time - and to allow the hosts to have access to the court of justice, to lodge a complaint and to bring their caregivers before the International Criminal Court.
Today, once again, everyone is in compassion, in emotion and rejoices in the liberation of these hostages.
But who says that these hostages might also have access to justice?
A court in The The Hague is designed for that.
'Whaving have you violated the rules of war, such as those of all the Geneva Conventions, we're going to bring you to court'?
