John Kerry conceivated, an unprecedented fact that American espionage “had gone too far inappropriately.”
John Kerry pointed to a softening of the United States’ defensive attitude toward its surveillance programs by admitting, in an unprecedented fact, that on several occasions espionage “had gone too far inappropriately.”
The Secretary of State has also admitted that, like Barack-O Obama, has been governed on “autopilot” when a series of incendiary whistleblowers by whistuncher Edward Snowden about NSA spying activities emerged.
Leaks put the US government at the center of a diplomatic storm with its allies.
Speaking at an intergovernmental conference in London via video link, Kerry said: “It is indisputable that the President, myself and other members of the government took note of certain things in autopilot mode because we had the opportunity, from the Second World War until the difficult years of the Cold-Wath, then of course 9/11.
It then became the first high-ranking member of the United States Government to admit that US espionage had gone beyond the bounds, but stressed the fact that everyone's rights had not been violated.
He said: “In some cases, it went too far inappropriately.”
And the president is determined to try to clarify and make it clear to the people that he was now carrying out an in-depth re-examination of these practices so that no one feels deceived.
I assure you that innocent people will not be victims of abuse as part of this process.
Mr. Kerry insisted, however, that the NSA was a force acting for the good and that its surveillance operations had saved many lives.
He added: “We live in a new world where people are poised.”
Radical extremism exists around the world which is determined to try to kill people, detonate them and attack Governments.
What if you could intercept their communications and stop them before they acted?
Indeed, we have prevented planes from falling, exaggerating buildings and people from being murdered because we were able to see the facts in advance.
In the meantime, US legislators will travel to Europe to address concerns about the alleged US espionage case and convince Europeans of the need to continue joint efforts to combat terrorism with the United States, the Chair of the Senate Subcommittee on European Affairs said on Thursday.
Senator Chris Murphy of Connecticut indicated that he had spoken before members of the European Parliament and others this week and that he was concerned about the threats they had made about a possible withdrawal of anti-terrorist organizations because of the frustration caused by the National Security Agency.
“It is really important for the protection of national security in the United States that Europeans stay with us in the mutual engagement in the fight against terrorism,” said Murphy, a Democrat serving his first term, and chairman of the US Senate Subcommittee on European Affairs, in an interview from Washington.
And I am going to go to Europe to make it clear that we must continue to work together to combat terrorism, despite the errance caused by NSA programmes.
The press reported that the NSA had captured millions of communications in Europe, which undermined relations with certain US allies, but the agency's director said this week that it was inaccurate and that it was a misunderstanding because it concerned metadata that the NATO allies had collected and shared with the United States.
Other revelations have documented by Snowden that the NSA has intercepted data and communications from the mobile phone of German Chancellor Angela Merkel and those of 34 other Heads of State.
Screening director James Clapper justified espionage of the Allegates by stating that it was necessary and a common practice on both sides.
In this turbulent atmosphere, Murphy indicated that his office was organizing a congressional trip, which should take place this year, and that he hoped that the delegation would include members from both parties and two chambers.
The names of other participating legislators should be unveiled in the coming days.
He indicated that the itinerary had yet to be determined.
Murphy stated that the purpose of the journey was to improve relations, but also to demonstrate “affectionate firmness”.
He explained that the European Heads of State must be honest with their citizens about the kind of espionage programmes that they themselves have been implementing for years.
“We can change our monitoring programmes to better protect the rights of Europeans, but they also need to understand that we are not the only ones in the world doing espionage,” said Murphy.
In the meantime, Kerry has planned to visit the Middle East and Poland this weekend to manage the resentment generated by US strategies in Syria, Egypt and Iran, as well as United States surveillance activities.
